window.harnessChunks["rows-3ba07934834b5eca.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-01T23:09:23Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yangtianspike/suisui-code\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SuisuiCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Yangtianspike/suisui-code/tree/c9017b0613b22151325a5904a521aefe4c62abbc\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision c9017b0613b22151325a5904a521aefe4c62abbc\">c9017b0 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform a coding objective into repository/process actions and a task result through iterative model-selected tool use with environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repository state, implementation alternatives, tool/process outcomes, context pressure, provider responses, permission outcomes and user corrections. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive coding/delegation/tool action and revise subsequent action from returned evidence. - Decision owner: the configured model actor in the first-party main Agent loop; spawned child Agent instances own the same task-local operational discretion for delegated S1 work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry/executor, permission engine, context manager and compaction, session persistence, iteration bounds, hooks, MCP adapters, TaskManager and worktree machinery. - Closure path: user objective/current context → model chooses a tool/action → SuisuiCode executes it → actual tool/environment result is appended to the conversation → the same model receives that result and chooses another action or a final answer. - Boundary reachability: suisuicode constructs this shipped provider-backed Agent directly; built-in coding tools and standard subagent runtime are wired by cli.py without requiring an external orchestration framework. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: if the model actor is removed while deterministic permissions, tool execution, context management and persistence remain, those mechanisms can enforce constraints but cannot choose open-ended coding actions. The substantive operational discretion is therefore agent-owned. - Evidence: README.md; src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; src/suisuicode/agent/runtocompletion.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model inference is supplied by an external provider. The finding credits SuisuiCode&#39;s first-party loop that places the model in the operational decision role, not provider-side internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate workspace interference among distinct delegated coding S1 cells by assigning an isolated Git worktree execution root to subagents whose standard definition requests worktree isolation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent coding agents operating against one checkout can overwrite or observe one another&#39;s in-progress file changes and lose clear ownership of a change set. - Distinct S1 units: the main Agent tool can instantiate multiple independent child Agent loops, including the shipped full-tool general-purpose role and fork/user/project roles, with separate conversations/runtime state and foreground/background lifecycles. - Inter-S1 disturbance: mutating child agents can otherwise act against the same repository workspace, coupling independent work through shared files and stale observations. - Attenuating coordination relation: Definition.isolation exposes the explicit worktree mode; AgentTool routes such a child through executewithworktree, which creates a dedicated worktree and runs the child&#39;s full model/tool loop under that worktree cwd. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the isolation result changes the actual filesystem root seen by that child; the injected worktree notice also requires local rereads before editing, so subsequent S1 file decisions/actions occur against the isolated checkout rather than the parent&#39;s live workspace. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is specifically an interference-attenuation mechanism for concurrent mutating S1s. Generic background delegation, TaskManager state and mailbox surfaces are not by themselves used as S2 evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: bind an operational child to a dedicated worktree so its mutable workspace is separated from other S1 work. - Decision owner: no shipped autonomous coordination actor owns that isolation choice as an organizational policy. The developer/operator selects it in the subagent definition; first-party runtime then deterministically enforces the relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagent-definition parser; worktree manager; unique worktree creation; withcwd; worktree context notice; auto-cleanup/preservation reporting. - Closure path: a standard subagent definition specifies isolation: worktree → the model delegates work to that role → SuisuiCode creates a dedicated worktree → the child Agent executes with that worktree as cwd → its later reads/writes remain separated from the parent&#39;s checkout → cleanup preserves or removes the isolated branch/worktree according to result state. - Boundary reachability: isolation: worktree is an explicit first-party definition field accepted from the standard user/project subagent-definition surfaces and executed by the ordinary model-facing Agent tool. The user supplies the role/configuration but does not have to invent the isolation path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the main model&#39;s discretion leaves the same definition-selected worktree relation available to any invocation; the decisive coordination policy is not autonomously selected by a shipped coordinator. The S2-specific constructor is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/subagent/definition.py; src/suisuicode/subagent/parser.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agentworktree.py; src/suisuicode/subagent/builtin/general-purpose.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: none of the inspected built-in role files makes worktree isolation the autonomous default; the positive finding is intentionally constructor-level. The frozen Team mode is not used to strengthen this state because its teammate execution path does not close reliably.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current population of delegated operational subagents by deciding which work to spawn, observing all background-run status/activity, cancelling work and re-tasking retained agents. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing workload decomposition, long-running or failed delegated work, redundant/obsolete current tasks, child-agent completion and the need to redirect follow-up work while the main objective is still active. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether/what to delegate, which child role/model/task to instantiate, whether a running task should be cancelled, and what follow-up task should be sent to a completed named agent. - Decision owner: the autonomous main model actor, because Agent, TaskList, TaskGet, TaskStop and SendMessage are registered as model-facing tools in the ordinary first-party composition. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TaskManager&#39;s task registry/status/result/activity accounting; background asyncio lifecycle; name registry; completion queue; tool schemas; deterministic cancel/restart execution. - Closure path: main model delegates via Agent → child Agent runs as an independent S1 under TaskManager → TaskManager records current status/activity/result → model can inspect the population through TaskList/TaskGet → model decides to wait, stop or issue follow-up work → TaskStop cancels or SendMessage restarts the retained child conversation, changing subsequent current operation. - Whole-system current view: TaskList returns all tracked background tasks, with id, name, status, tool count and last activity; TaskGet adds result/error, timing and usage. At the declared delegated-subagent recursion this is a current view of the operational child population managed by the main agent. - Current-control decision scope: the main model can create operational commitments through Agent, terminate an active commitment through TaskStop, inspect completion/failure, and assign follow-up work through SendMessage; these decisions alter which S1 work remains active and what retained agents do next. - Boundary reachability: cli.py registers the Agent, TaskList, TaskGet, TaskStop and SendMessage tools in the ordinary shipped main-agent registry, and AgentTool/TaskManager instantiate and track the same first-party child Agent loops; no Team-mode path or application-authored supervisor is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: TaskManager itself only records/enforces lifecycle transitions. Without the main model actor, the registry can list or cancel only preselected ids but does not decide which current work should exist, be stopped or be re-tasked. The substantive supervisory discretion belongs to the model. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agenttool.py; src/suisuicode/task/manager.py; src/suisuicode/task/tools.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: this S3 finding is the ordinary functional SubAgent/TaskManager composition, not the richer advertised Coordinator/Team composition. SendMessage resumes completed named agents rather than modifying an already-running child&#39;s prompt; a currently wrong run is instead stopped and replaced/redelegated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party complementary operational-audit loop with sufficiently independent access, audit judgment and corrective return is established at the assessed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission checks, hooks, tests, worktree isolation and lead-side result inspection provide safety/visibility, but none closes a materially independent audit channel over current operational S1 claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct first-party auditor is shown owning an independent approve/reject/escalate judgment over the operating subagent population. - Decision owner: not established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission engine; lifecycle hooks; task result/status inspection; worktree cleanup; ordinary user review; repository tests. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Claim being audited: no distinct operational claim is assigned to an independent first-party auditor in the standard runtime. - Ordinary reporting path: child results/status/tool activity return through TaskManager and ordinary model/tool results to the same main supervisory agent. - Complementary access path: not established. Hooks are configurable extension points supplied by operator/project configuration, while worktree and permission checks are inline execution/support mechanisms rather than a separate route to operational reality. - Independence boundary: no qualifying shipped autonomous auditor with materially different access from the ordinary lead/task path was found. Development tests and user review remain outside runtime S3 ownership. - Who acts on findings: the main model or human can react to normal task/tool results; there is no distinct S3 finding-and-return closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: autonomous main/subagents can inspect work, but the same operating/supervisory actors do not become a complementary audit function merely by reviewing their own outputs. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/task/tools.py; src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a downstream project can configure hooks or specialist agents to perform review, but generic extensibility does not establish first-party S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: convert durable project/user distinctions learned from completed work into model-selected long-term operating guidance that changes later SuisuiCode sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: recurring user corrections, stable preferences, project architecture/conventions, ongoing work/decisions and reference knowledge that later tasks would otherwise need to rediscover or could repeatedly mishandle. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether observed information is worth retaining long term and whether to create, update, merge or delete project/user notes, including what How to apply guidance future work should receive. - Decision owner: the autonomous model actor invoked by the first-party memory-update loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: asynchronous per-turn trigger; existing-index/note retrieval; JSON action parser; project/user Store persistence; MEMORY.md index; startup loadindex; system-prompt assembly. - Closure path: a main coding turn completes → Agent.run extracts the recent turn and schedules memory.Manager.updateasync → memory model compares recent evidence with existing memory and selects create/update/delete actions → deterministic stores persist the chosen long-term guidance → a later SuisuiCode startup loads the memory index → that memory enters the main Agent system prompt and changes subsequent operating context. - Boundary reachability: the memory Manager is constructed by the standard CLI, passed into the main Agent, and invoked automatically after completed main turns when a provider is configured; persisted memory is loaded again by the same standard CLI path. - External distinction: actual conversation/project evidence includes user corrections/preferences and project knowledge learned during repository-facing work rather than only internal scheduler state. - Future / prospective distinction: the updater is explicitly told to retain only information worth remembering long term; feedback is framed as information to remember to avoid the same future error, while project notes encode ongoing goals/conventions and future How to apply guidance. - Adaptation option generated: the memory model autonomously generates structured create, update or delete actions and the content/type/level of each durable note, merging old and new evidence when appropriate. - Path back into current capability / S3: persisted notes are indexed by the memory stores; cli.py loads that index at startup and passes it as memorytext to the main Agent, whose system prompt uses it on later coding work. The returned adaptation therefore changes the information and operating guidance available to present S1/S3 decisions in later sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic stores apply a structured action, but they do not decide what is strategically worth remembering, whether old guidance is obsolete, or how future work should apply a distinction. Removing the memory model leaves persistence machinery but no adaptation judgment. - Evidence: src/suisuicode/agent/agent.py; src/suisuicode/memory/manager.py; src/suisuicode/memory/prompts.py; src/suisuicode/cli.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the freshly written memory is not shown being hot-reloaded into the already-running main Agent&#39;s cached memorytext; the established return path is durable adaptation into later startup/session operation, which is sufficient for the prospective loop but should not be read as same-turn adaptation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy tension-and-resolution loop is established at the chosen recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permissions, modes, system prompts, subagent definitions, coordinator instructions and memory constrain or inform operation, but none is shown resolving a dispute about the organization&#39;s identity or ultimate policy. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission modes/rules; user approvals; configuration; system/subagent prompts; Coordinator feature flag; memory/instructions; hook policies. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models make task, supervisory and memory-adaptation decisions, but no first-party actor is given ultimate authority to resolve identity/policy tensions and return such a resolution into subsequent organization-wide operation. - Evidence: README.md; src/suisuicode/cli.py; src/suisuicode/coordinator/init.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: human control over permissions or feature/configuration choices is ordinary parent/operator control over implementation and task execution; it is not promoted to S5 without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/8a372087e865279eca3307563c52720f7ad8c4b2/assessments/suisuicode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 8a372087e865279eca3307563c52720f7ad8c4b2\">8a37208 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T00:36:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zybuu-ai/abhed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Abhed</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/zybuu-ai/abhed/tree/151b8ddceee7ae232e526b3c237356151dde780e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 151b8ddceee7ae232e526b3c237356151dde780e\">151b8dd &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: transform an open-ended technical objective into repository/environment outcomes through repeated model-selected actions, tool execution and evidence feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing user requirements, repository/filesystem/process state, tool results/errors, model/provider responses, policy outcomes, context pressure and child-agent results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive response/tool action and revise later behavior from observed tool/environment evidence. - Decision owner: the active Abhed model agent in the primary or delegated child loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Loop, tool registry, policy/approver, session workspace, event recorder/store, context offload/compaction, sandbox, provider adapter, budgets and presentation/server surfaces. - Closure path: objective enters the session → model selects an action/tool call → Abhed policy/runtime executes or refuses it → result/denial is recorded and returned to the conversation → later model turn observes changed evidence and chooses the next action → task/environment state changes. - Boundary reachability: app/main.go wires the standard interactive/headless Community Edition session directly to Loop and its tool surface; spawned subagents instantiate the same first-party loop with bounded role-specific configuration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code executes, constrains and records already-selected actions, but removing the model eliminates the substantive next-action choice and adaptive interpretation of returned evidence. - Evidence: internal/agent/loop.go; app/main.go; internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive write interference between distinct concurrently executing child agents by separating editing work into isolated git worktrees/branches when parallel writes would collide. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more writing subagents operating in one checkout can overwrite one another, obscure which changes survived and make concurrent implementation state inconsistent. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide whether a concrete parallel batch requires worktree isolation rather than a shared workspace and supply the task decomposition/roles executed under that coordination choice. - Decision owner: the primary Abhed model agent calling the standard tasks tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tasks schema/description, git worktree creation, per-child SubagentRequest.Workspace, child session scoping, branch naming, concurrency semaphore and returned per-worktree diff/merge instructions. - Closure path: primary agent identifies independent parallel work with write risk → selects tasks with isolation: &quot;worktree&quot; → runtime creates one branch/worktree per child and binds each child S1 to its own workspace → children operate without cross-writing → tasks returns every child summary plus branch/change/merge facts → parent uses that coordinated result for subsequent integration or further work. - Boundary reachability: app/main.go registers agent.Tasks in the normal Community Edition tool registry and supplies the standard SubagentFactory; isolation is a documented model-callable argument rather than a test-only helper. - Distinct S1 units: two or more fresh-context child Loop instances spawned concurrently by one tasks call, each capable of model/tool operation on a bounded delegated outcome. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel writing children sharing one checkout can overwrite one another&#39;s changes so the parent cannot reliably tell whose change survived. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-selected worktree isolation gives every child a separate git branch/checkout and filesystem scope, preventing one child from modifying another child&#39;s current work or the main tree. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: workspace binding changes each child S1&#39;s operational environment before it acts; after completion the parent receives per-branch status/change/merge information and can choose how subsequent integration work proceeds. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on an explicit inter-S1 write-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation relation built specifically to attenuate it; concurrency limits and subagent delegation alone are not credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Abhed deterministically enforces the requested isolation, but the parent model owns the task-specific judgment that concurrent writing work needs isolation and invokes the coordinating mode. Removing that judgment leaves the mechanism but not the actual coordination decision for the current task. - Evidence: internal/agent/parallel.go; internal/agent/paralleltest.go; app/main.go; internal/agent/subagent.go. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: isolation: &quot;none&quot; deliberately permits shared-workspace operation. S2=A credits the standard agent-owned coordination path when parallel writes require isolation; it does not claim every concurrent batch is automatically coordinated.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party whole-system inside-and-now current-control loop is established over the temporary Abhed multi-agent organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Abhed bounds spend, spawn count and concurrency and the parent chooses initial delegation, but no standard model-callable path manages changing live child commitments from a whole-system current view. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for ongoing cross-child current control. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: parent-created task batch, per-child maxturns, hierarchical token/subagent limits, MaxParallel, context cancellation and final aggregated task outcomes constrain execution but do not themselves establish S3. - Closure path: not applicable; the parent launches delegated work and receives results after child completion, but no reviewed path closes live whole-system status → discretionary intervention/reallocation → renewed whole-system feedback while children are active. - Whole-system current view: not established. task blocks on one child; tasks launches the requested batch and waits for all goroutines before assembling the returned outcome report. - Current-control decision scope: the parent can choose initial tasks, roles, turn caps and isolation, but the standard tool surface exposes no child list/progress/steer/interrupt/close/reassign controls over the running batch. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without an established S3 function, ownership classification does not proceed. Initial decomposition and deterministic limit enforcement remain S1/S2 support rather than a whole-system current regulator. - Evidence: internal/agent/subagent.go; internal/agent/parallel.go; config/config.go; app/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: the architecture document calls the primary actor an orchestrator and describes plan/budget ownership, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires the actual current-control function; naming, initial delegation and configured resource caps are insufficient.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently inspect current repository reality for correctness defects through a fresh-context read-only reviewer child and return evidence-backed findings for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: implementation defects or inaccurate/incomplete conclusions that the implementing primary/child agent&#39;s normal execution and self-report may fail to expose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: form a correctness judgment from direct read/search access to the workspace and identify concrete bugs/failure scenarios independently of the implementer&#39;s transcript or summary. - Decision owner: the model running the built-in review subagent profile. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fresh subagent context, review profile instruction, read-only read/glob/grep subset, independent child Loop, bounded summary return and parent model continuation. - Closure path: implementation/current repository state exists → parent invokes a review child through task or tasks → reviewer independently reads/searches repository artifacts and forms correctness findings → bounded findings return to the parent as a tool result → parent can revise code, test, delegate or otherwise change subsequent operation from those findings. - Boundary reachability: review is a built-in first-party profile exposed through the normal task / tasks tool schemas, and those tools are registered in the ordinary Community Edition CLI/headless runtime. - Claim being audited: that the current implementation/repository state produced or accepted by ordinary agent operation is correct enough to proceed without a concrete correctness defect. - Ordinary reporting path: an implementing agent/subagent returns its own assistant summary and normal tool/session record to the parent/runtime. - Complementary access path: a separate review child starts with a fresh context and directly inspects current workspace files through read/search tools rather than consuming only the implementing worker&#39;s summary. - Independence boundary: the reviewer receives none of the parent&#39;s conversational turns, has an explicit non-fixing review role and a read-only tool subset; its correctness judgment is produced by a distinct child model loop over repository evidence. - Who acts on findings: the primary Abhed model agent receives the reviewer summary as a normal tool result and owns subsequent corrective tool use, testing or further delegation within the same session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Abhed supplies the separate review channel and tool restrictions, but the substantive bug judgment is made by the reviewer model from direct evidence. Removing reviewer-agent judgment leaves read/search primitives but no equivalent correctness findings. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompt.go; internal/agent/subagent.go; app/main.go; docs/guide/15-hawkeye.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: HawkEYE and repository eval/red-team infrastructure are not the decisive S3 owner. The positive claim is the standard fresh-context direct-workspace reviewer path; arbitrary use of a generic subagent as a reviewer would not have been sufficient without this built-in role/access boundary.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party outside-and-then organizational adaptation loop is established at the assessed session/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Abhed can retrieve current information, search the web, use skills, switch models and retain session/history state, but these capabilities are not shown owning prospective adaptation of the harness organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for adopting an organizational capability/policy change from external/future distinctions. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search, RAG/retrieval, skills, provider/model selection, project memory, session replay/fork/recall, compaction and repository-development eval/benchmark material. - Closure path: not applicable; reviewed paths acquire information or change task execution/configuration but do not close external/future sensing → adaptation option → adopted change to current organizational capability/S3. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model may autonomously research information for an S1 task, but no separate S4 organizational adaptation function is established, so task research is not reclassified as S4. - Evidence: internal/agent/prompt.go; app/main.go; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party identity/ultimate-policy decision loop is established at the assessed session/deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: system prompt, project/org memory, permission modes, deny/ask/allow rules, managed config and approval gates constrain operational behavior but are not shown resolving identity/ultimate-policy issues. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for an identity-level policy issue. - Decision owner: none established for S5 within the Community Edition runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: layered system prompt/memory, managed configuration precedence, ordered policy engine, absolute deny, destructive-action confirmation, human approver and workspace/additional-directory boundaries. - Closure path: not applicable; no reviewed path detects an identity/ultimate-policy issue, escalates it to legitimate ultimate authority and returns an authoritative resolution that governs subsequent operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents act within operator/org-configured constraints and can receive ordinary action approvals/denials, but neither static policy nor generic approval confers ultimate policy authority on the model or operator as an S5 loop. - Evidence: docs/guide/04-permissions.md; config/config.go; internal/agent/prompt.go; app/main.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review - Confidence: high - Caveats: managed org policy can be stronger than local configuration and interactive approval can return reasons to the model, but Methodology 0.3.6 does not treat constraint precedence or ordinary action authorization as S5 without identity/ultimate-policy closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/609935e136cc3df4e59276eb2d9baf5b866c4334/assessments/abhed.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 609935e136cc3df4e59276eb2d9baf5b866c4334\">609935e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T22:55:14+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rootkiller6788/dsh-flow\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">dsh-flow</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/rootkiller6788/dsh-flow/tree/d11ad4f04400fda9c58cf2b9b5a11fa971818eed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d11ad4f04400fda9c58cf2b9b5a11fa971818eed\">d11ad4f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-goal work in the actual project environment through multiple scoped member model-agent sessions; captain may also carry a bounded task, but its principal credited role here is team control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varying implementation/research/verifier tasks, changing file/tool outputs, failed commands, partial results and review findings. - Decisive decision or feedback right: selected member model chooses tool/actions and reports task outcome within the assigned contract; the host supplies the model/tool engine, the plugin supplies live delegation, scoped task/attempt identity and return to team. - Decision owner: the operating captain/member model sessions, instantiated as DSH host continuable agents for this plugin&#39;s selected team; deterministic scheduler, rules and adapter do not choose the semantic work solution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: createSubagentsRunner, host continuable child API, model route inheritance, task claims/attempt capability, assigned dependency results, inbox, state logs and completion evidence gate. - Closure path: approved team → scheduler dispatches ready task to member session → model acts in workspace → flowupdatetask and message return structured evidence → team state/captain observes → next work or review task. The host executes individual tools; first-party dsh-flow installs the closed team feedback relation. - Boundary reachability: runner: subagents is the default mount and /dsh-flow plus flowapprove initiate actual member execution, with tests exercising scheduler/host adapters. Manual mode is expressly non-executing. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the member&#39;s model decision there is no task-specific implementation/verification result; locks, dispatch and signatures alone would only manage empty work tickets. - Evidence: subagents runner mounting host agent, scheduler claim/dispatch, assignment prompt/tool output, supported team command. - Basis: structural, supported by fake-host scheduler/subagent tests. - Confidence: high for first-party team wiring, medium-high for external host runtime reachability without a live DSH model session. - Caveats: host/model provider remains separate; a pure runner: manual installation has no active S1, and an imported .agent-teams record is read-only visualization rather than new execution.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate destructive interference among multiple member S1 cells attempting incompatible writes or dependent work during a shared team goal. - Disturbance / variety regulated: file write collision, duplicate task claiming, unfinished attempt interleaving and premature dependent dispatch between separately operating team members. - Distinct S1 units: independently operating, named member model sessions with separate task/attempt identities and potentially competing paths in one workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two independently acting implementation/repair members may claim overlapping workspace paths and overwrite each other&#39;s output; two task claims can also invalidate one member&#39;s existing attempt. - Specific interference / oscillation: concurrently open implementation/repair tasks can declare overlapping inScope paths and corrupt/overwrite each other&#39;s work; duplicate task claims or a member taking a second task before finishing its first can interleave commitments and lose the correct attempt. - Attenuating coordination relation: the shared first-party task graph refuses overlapping live writer scopes, withholds dependent tasks, and serializes member claims with identity-bound attempts. - Coordination response: validateCreateTask rejects overlapping scopes and explains serialize these tasks or split the paths; dependency readiness blocks downstream work until predecessor success; createMemberQueues and task locks ensure one active claim per member/attempt. Reassignment revalidates handoff after interruption. - Decisive decision or feedback right: first-party deterministic rules decide that an incompatible graph/task claim is inadmissible; captain/member may then revise work scopes or dependencies after the error, but the enforced anti-collision verdict is constructor-owned, so the state is C rather than A. - Decision owner: shipped rule constructors gates.js/assignment.js plus scheduler serialization for the S2 verdict; model captain remains owner of a revised plan, not of the invariant. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: scope-overlap matcher, team lock, member serial queue, dependency graph, attempt ID/capability, delivery state and error response. - Closure path: proposed overlapping tasks/competing claim → deterministic rejection or blocked readiness → visible tool error/status to captain and members → corrected serial/decomposed plan or later ready dispatch; later task work respects the new non-overlapping graph. This is a concrete interference/attenuation path, not generic messaging. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: the captain receives the explicit scope-conflict error and must split/serialize the plan; after a dependency finishes, the scheduler makes only newly eligible nonconflicting work available to the next member turn. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: a concrete overlapping-writer interference is recognized and prevented at the team boundary, with refusal and corrected task eligibility affecting what distinct S1 cells can actually do next. - Boundary reachability: gates and claim selection are invoked by normal flowcreatetask/flowupdatetask and default subagents scheduler, not only a test fixture. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model from the overlap test leaves the same deterministic reject/serialized admission decision; model-generated alternatives support adaptation but the decisive S2 collision protection is constructed in code. - Evidence: live overlapping writers refused, dependency/owned-task selection, per-member double-claim lock, task reassignment locking. - Basis: explicit code-level invariant and structural execution path. - Confidence: high for deterministic protection; not a measurement of all real-world file races. - Caveats: scopes rely on accurate captain/model-declared paths; unmodeled side effects and external writers remain outside these protections. A blocked task requires legitimate correction; the gate alone does not invent the right new plan.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: let a team captain regulate the whole currently operating group—member commitments, task ownership, progression, failures and interventions—while members absorb local implementation variety. - Whole-system current view: flowstatus supplies all active team members, their live activity, all task assignments/dependencies/attempts/outputs, captain mail and quality/coverage state to the captain&#39;s model context. - Current-control decision scope: captain model chooses new/reassigned current member commitments, can halt or resume this team, send corrective instructions and change the task graph based on the complete status/failure feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent or stuck members, stale attempts, work failures, dependency bottlenecks, incomplete coverage, quality escalation and changing current priorities within an approved goal. - Decisive decision or feedback right: captain model can see all members/task statuses/mailboxes and choose current task creation, assignment/reassignment, messages, halt/resume and follow-up based on returned work. Human approval of the initial staged plan is a bounded launch right, not automatically a distinct S3 parent mode. - Decision owner: the model-backed captain session for semantic whole-team current interventions; deterministic scheduler selects ready tasks and enforces locks/attempt identity; operator chooses profile/approval policy and is the final deployment authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: flowstatus whole-team view, per-member flowsendmessage, flowreassigntask with interrupt, flowcreatetask, flowhalt/flowresume, membership controls, state/coverage diagnostics, event-driven scheduler and mailboxes. - Closure path: member progress/failure → persisted task state and captain-directed message (live steer or mailbox) → captain sees all task commitments and evidence in flowstatus → model chooses intervention/reassignment/repair/stop → gated tool writes state and scheduler dispatches changed work → subsequent member outcomes return again. - Boundary reachability: selected default subagents mode creates an actual captain-owned team, dispatches members and registers captain-facing tools; live message steering and durable fallback make the feedback available in normal supported operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: scheduler/quality gates can enforce the captain&#39;s selected commitments, but cannot decide whether to change a goal-constrained priority, replace an implementer or ask for revised evidence. That discretionary current-control choice resides in the captain model when operated as a team. - Evidence: captain-only lifecycle tools, whole-team status, live member→captain steer, task ownership/interrupt and reassignment, scheduler feedback. - Basis: structural supported-mode control loop, with explicit tool rights and return path. - Confidence: medium-high for model-visible group view/control; no live provider captain intervention was measured. - Caveats: a pure manual runner is an operator editing a static plan, not autonomous S3. A single all-in-one worker task cannot be promoted to a whole-team S3 merely because a captain persona exists.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: obtain a separate reviewer model&#39;s judgment on implementation/repair claims using task files, acceptance evidence and changed paths, and route findings back to corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: divergence between an implementer&#39;s completion assertion and raw code/test artifacts, including unresolved severe findings and incorrect acceptance or changed-path claims. - Claim being audited: an implementation or repair member&#39;s reported completion, scope compliance, tested behavior and asserted acceptance against the actual delivered artifacts. - Ordinary reporting path: implementer flowupdatetask status/output/changedPaths/acceptanceResults/commandsRun plus ordinary member message to the captain. - Complementary access path: a separately assigned review member receives a structured kind: review task with reviewedTaskId, independent prompt/work and access to code/verification artifacts under host task tools, then returns its own verdict and structured findings with concrete required fixes. - Independence boundary: in the selected structured quality mode, the reviewer is a different member model session from the implementer; automatic follow-up deliberately chooses a reviewer other than the repair implementer. This is task-relative independence, not an external security audit. - Decisive decision or feedback right: reviewer model inspects evidence and makes pass, needsrevision or reject judgment; pure rules validate the result shape and plan bounded repairs, while captain/user can act on escalations. - Decision owner: independent reviewer member model for the substantive audit judgment; constructor-owned evaluateQualityCompletion and planQualityFollowUp enforce nonfabrication of completion and deterministic follow-up limits; human/captain control task policies. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: structured review task kind/objective/acceptance, required verdict/findings with problem/requiredFix/optional file/line, task-specific claim and role permissions, changed-path/verification gates, repair/review round counters and team status/canvas. - Closure path: implementer output + actual code/evidence → separately executed reviewer task → model verdict with findings → flowupdatetask rejects unsupported pass or accepts failure → planQualityFollowUp creates scoped repair and fresh independent review (or escalates) → member performs repair and reviewer rechecks → captain sees updated current quality state. - Boundary reachability: standard flowcreatetask accepts review tasks, default active subagents runner dispatches them as ordinary member work, flowupdatetask carries verdict/findings and the first-party follow-up rule selects a distinct reviewer. This is selectable first-party functionality, not a standalone benchmark evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the evidence comparison and diagnosis is model-owned by a distinct reviewer, not produced solely by the implementer or a deterministic unit test. The shape validator and automatic repair planner do not confer audit judgment ownership. - Who acts on findings: an implementation member performs planned repair; another reviewer performs the new review; captain receives status/escalation and can reallocate current work or ask the user when limits are reached. - Evidence: review-task schema and real dispatch, review evidence parsing, completion review verdict checks, separate reviewer and repair round, review assignment prompt. - Basis: structurally evidenced selected-mode audit with model-authored reviewer verdict; not inferred from generic QA label. - Confidence: medium-high for path/role separation, medium for observed real-artifact review quality without live provider. - Caveats: the captain/profile must actually create/assign a separate reviewer task; ordinary work has no mandatory review. The initial schema does not universally forbid a captain from assigning an ordinary review to the same implementer, so S3=A refers to a distinct reviewer mode and explicit automatic follow-up separation, not every team or task.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped whole-team, externally and prospectively oriented agent loop constructs future adaptation options and closes them against current S3 capacity. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task progress, plan defects, implementation/test failures, member availability, required scope changes and review follow-ups; these are present goal/work controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: captain model revises the current staged or active task plan; human/deployment author configures roles/profiles and decides whether the team should pursue a new goal. - Decision owner: captain for current work, operator/higher organization for long-horizon goals and profile evolution; no first-party future-environment intelligence owner found. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: profile registry, plan requirements, goal coverage, task DAG, status/coverage matrix, review findings and imported team sources. - Closure path: task failure/review updates current repair or work; no separate external trend/opportunity sensing → modeled alternatives → S3–S4 dialogue → changed long-term capability path is shipped. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task planning and immediate code repair are not S4 even when model-driven; roster/configurability is not strategic external adaptation. - Evidence: profile/task-planning modes, bounded repair follow-up, captain tool boundary. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high across reviewed supported modes. - Caveats: the plugin can host a research task about future technologies, but a one-off task is an S1 operation until a governed prospective capability-change loop is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy deliberation and returned governance path is established for the deployed team as an ongoing viable organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: team membership, task ownership, profile review rules, host permissions, approval of staged plan and risk/quality limits are ordinary task controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: human operator/deployment author chooses goals/profiles and approves a staged plan; captain model edits a plan within those granted rights; host identity/permission checks protect sessions. - Decision owner: human/external DSH host for ultimate organizational identity and policy; kernel enforces profiles, team session role and task capability. No explicit first-party S5 judgment at an ultimate identity tension is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: flowapprove staging gate, captain/member access restrictions, persisted identity/team IDs, member retirement guard, review policy, scope and acceptance constraints. - Closure path: approved plan activates new member work and constraints, but that is normal task launch/current-control authorization, not a distinct identity-level issue routed to legitimate parent S5 and returned as ultimate policy. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human approval of an ordinary plan and profile YAML do not automatically become parent-governed S5; a captain role label is not ultimate authority. - Evidence: staged approval and tool rights, captain/member tool distinction, retired member guard. - Basis: structural negative. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a human organization using the plugin may provide real higher-recursion governance, but the plugin&#39;s task approval alone does not demonstrate the required S5 function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4d0c2bbcb966bc026eba64cf8652da2cc1922f17/assessments/ds/dsh-flow.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4d0c2bbcb966bc026eba64cf8652da2cc1922f17\">4d0c2bb &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T00:29:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HarnessDesk/HarnessDesk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HarnessDesk</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-01\">2026-10-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/HarnessDesk/HarnessDesk/tree/24add77185eebe321b0aa0b578933acf4a8e05d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 24add77185eebe321b0aa0b578933acf4a8e05d3\">24add77 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing engineering work as a HarnessDesk-seated autonomous coding-agent cell and return changed artifacts, outcomes and evidence into the desk. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific repository state, implementation alternatives, tool/test feedback, runtime capability differences, blockers and evidence encountered while producing a software outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose and revise the next task-specific coding, inspection or tool action from the objective and returned environment state. - Decision owner: the supported autonomous coding-agent session seated through HarnessDesk, including a first-party Agent role instantiated on a compatible Codex/ACP runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent briefs/seating, adapter negotiation, transcripts, plugin tools, approvals/permission ceilings, worktrees, task/context state and host persistence. - Closure path: task enters seated conversation → agent inspects/acts through its runtime → repository/tool feedback returns → same agent revises, continues or completes → HarnessDesk persists outcome/context/evidence. - Boundary reachability: Agent definitions, seating, adapters, Room membership and tool projection ship in the standard distribution; no application-authored orchestration is required to instantiate the supported worker cell. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the autonomous worker while leaving host state, worktrees, board and adapters leaves enforcement/persistence but no actor making the open-ended engineering decisions. - Evidence: README.md; docs/architecture.md; docs/agents.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: internal delegation/planning/memory of Codex, Claude, Cursor, Gemini or other external runtimes is not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive interference among concurrent coding cells sharing tasks/files and prevent recursive peer-message chatter from destabilizing the Room. - Disturbance / variety regulated: overlapping edits or claims on the same task/path, unsafe concurrent work over shared repository state, and uncontrolled reciprocal agent messaging. - Distinct S1 units: two or more independently running HarnessDesk-seated coding-agent conversations in one Room/project, potentially on different vendor runtimes. - Inter-S1 disturbance: two cells can attempt the same card or overlapping files, or wake one another repeatedly through peer messages. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic Board claims reject held intents, unfinished dependencies and overlapping file globs; leases expose stranded claims; worktrees isolate races; Channel delivery is attributed, rate/duplicate/queue limited and awakened replies are not forwarded back automatically. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: conflict/refusal/claim results are returned to the requesting agent, which chooses another task/path, waits, releases, communicates, uses isolation or escalates; dependency context is returned to later claimants. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation targets concrete inter-worker collision modes—file/task contention and message oscillation—and closes explicit refusal/return paths that change later worker behaviour. - Decisive decision or feedback right: after host conflict feedback, autonomous participating agents choose the mutual-adjustment response; the host deterministically enforces exclusion and loop constraints. - Decision owner: participating autonomous coding-agent cells. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Board transactions, file-glob conflict checks, leases, worktrees, dependency gating, delivery states, loop guards, context packages and host audit state. - Closure path: agents claim intended work → host accepts or rejects incompatible ownership → result returns to agent → agent changes work/communication or proceeds → release/completion changes the shared state seen by peers. - Boundary reachability: Board/team tools are projected to supported Codex and ACP agents; host caller correlation rejects unattributed calls, so the coordination loop is available without custom external glue. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic exclusion could still block a conflict without the agent, but it would not choose the organizational response to that conflict. That discretion is held by the autonomous participant. - Evidence: docs/multi-agent.md; docs/architecture.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: claim enforcement regulates supported Board/worktree participants, not unrelated out-of-band processes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C(P) - Function: regulate the current Room as a whole by choosing roles/permissions, moving current work between cells, reacting to round outcomes and reserving irreversible steps for a selected authority. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current mismatch among work cells—who should perform which role, whether rejected work returns to implementation, what a seat may publish/merge, and when current work must stop, change owner or reach a person. - Whole-system current view: Room Board/roster/channel and Flow run expose current members, claims, role-addressed cards, outcomes and run state across the participating S1 cells. - Current-control decision scope: whole-Room allocation, role/permission assignment, guarded transitions between current work rounds, intervention on blocked/rejected work and ownership of irreversible merge-like actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise those current roles, commitments and transitions on behalf of the Room rather than merely execute one local task. - Decision owner: base constructor mode—developer/Flow author chooses the policy and deterministic Flow engine enforces it; parent mode—human Room referee owns current assignment/intervention/irreversible-step choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Flow parser/validator, role seating, permission ceilings, outcome vocabulary, guarded first-match transitions, frozen run policy, Board state, person-role cards and stop/restart semantics. - Closure path: Room/round state reaches predeclared Flow policy or human referee → current-control decision selects next role/card/permission/intervention → Flow/Board machinery changes subsequent S1 work. - Boundary reachability: Flow is a supported first-party project format with runnable shipped examples; ordinary Rooms expose the human referee path directly in the local standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Flow engine enforces a preselected whole-Room policy but does not autonomously choose/revise that policy from current variety; ordinary Room documentation explicitly assigns the referee right to the human. No autonomous S3 supervisor is established. - Evidence: docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; .harnessdesk/flows/fix-and-review.yml. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: deterministic transitions do not own S3 discretion, and a reviewer/judge is not promoted to S3 merely because it controls one edge.\">C(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: challenge an implementation cell&#39;s ordinary completion claim through independent autonomous inspection of primary artifacts and return findings into corrective work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty that the fixer&#39;s own completion/publication claim corresponds to a correct change rather than merely a plausible self-report. - Claim being audited: that a published fixer change is correct and suitable to proceed. - Ordinary reporting path: fixer publishes its own card, note/context and may report checks or success. - Complementary access path: shipped fix-and-review opens three separate reviewer-agent seats; each is ordered to inspect the pull-request diff and surrounding code independently and not read other reviewers&#39; answers. - Independence boundary: reviewers are distinct autonomous seats from the fixer, receive the produced change rather than the fixer&#39;s hidden reasoning, and are explicitly isolated from one another&#39;s judgments for the review round. - Who acts on findings: the Flow returns any request-changes findings to the fixer, which must answer them and republish; unanimous approval alone advances to the human merge step. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge from independent artifact inspection whether the implementation claim withstands review and identify defects requiring correction. - Decision owner: the autonomous reviewer agents own approve versus request-changes; the deterministic engine only validates/collects outcomes and applies the return rule. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: reviewer role seating, addressed cards, outcome validation, context packages, Flow rules, check/diff/PR/CI evidence, commit-bound freshness and stale-evidence downgrading. - Closure path: fixer publishes → independent reviewers inspect raw artifacts → judgments/findings return → any negative finding reopens fixer work → corrected publication is reviewed again. - Boundary reachability: fix-and-review.yml is committed as a runnable HarnessDesk Flow and standard Agent/seat/Flow machinery opens the reviewer actors locally. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing reviewer agents while retaining deterministic Flow/evidence machinery leaves no actor making the independent semantic judgment; check/CI facts support rather than replace that judgment. - Evidence: .harnessdesk/flows/fix-and-review.yml; docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; docs/agents.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is a supported Flow mode, not a claim that every ordinary Room automatically audits every S1 result.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible future changes in models, runtimes, user needs, CI events, schedules, hosted execution and tool capabilities were inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established that turns sensed external/future distinctions into adaptation options and returns an adaptation choice into current HarnessDesk capability. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at the deployed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: runtime capability negotiation, local runtime/version discovery, usage dashboards, repository VISION/roadmap and current Flow/Room state. - Closure path: no supported outside-and-then adaptation decision-and-return loop exists at the frozen ref. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current capability discovery and runtime selection react to present machine state; they do not model a future environment and develop adaptation options. Roadmap/market reasoning is adjacent development governance. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; VISION.md; docs/data-boundaries.md; docs/flows.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: PR/CI/timer/webhook-triggered and hosted work are named as future directions, but current Flow triggers are manual and those lanes are not built.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established for the selected HarnessDesk project/Room organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: possible tension over ultimate organizational purpose, identity, trust boundary or policy requiring highest-level closure was inspected. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established beyond ordinary permission ceilings, approvals, Flow policy, local data settings and current-control decisions. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at the reviewed deployed boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent briefs/permission ceilings, common approval policy, plugin permission gates, human referee, Flow files, local-only data defaults, VISION promises and settings. - Closure path: no first-party runtime path was found where an identity/ultimate-policy issue reaches legitimate ultimate authority and the returned decision governs subsequent operation as S5. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agents do not own ultimate HarnessDesk identity/policy, but absence of agent ownership does not create parent S5; ordinary approval, merge, role permission and Flow edit remain operational/current-control matters. - Evidence: docs/agents.md; docs/flows.md; docs/multi-agent.md; VISION.md; docs/data-boundaries.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: public promises such as vendor neutrality/local-data defaults are maintainer/development governance surfaces and are not borrowed as a deployed Room&#39;s S5 owner.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3f74e33b03b91b79ed7e360fbc1eae4d60dcf2c4/assessments/harnessdesk.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3f74e33b03b91b79ed7e360fbc1eae4d60dcf2c4\">3f74e33 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T01:05:54+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gurp-S/XEYO\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">XEYO</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Gurp-S/XEYO/tree/5a41ffb430549e6871e09f81be26f41299b423d8\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5a41ffb430549e6871e09f81be26f41299b423d8\">5a41ffb &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate and modify a workspace in pursuit of a user task using model-selected tools and feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing file content, differing user requests, tool errors, refused permissions, process failures, stale work and uncertain task solutions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose subsequent Read/Grep/Glob/Edit/Write/Bash and other supported operations after tool results, or choose delegation and synthesis. - Decision owner: parent model through queryloop, and independent model loops in runsubagent / worktree task sessions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool registry, permission checks, query-engine budget/session lifecycle, sidechain journal and deterministic write limits. - Closure path: task and tool observations → model chooses an operation → first-party registry executes or rejects it → factual tool result updates next model turn. - Boundary reachability: SessionPool builds the standard registry and model runtime; AgentTool creates child query loops. Separate coord worktree runs use sessionworkfn only on explicitly invoked CLI execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: host code restricts execution, but it does not select task-specific coding strategies or repair actions independently of the model. - Evidence: queryloop.py; queryengine.py; catalog.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: model providers are external inference dependencies and were not exercised in this assessment; Python module compilation does not establish live agent task success.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: regulate concurrent or overlapping task and workspace commitments among distinct child/root S1 actors. - Disturbance / variety regulated: simultaneous child writes, stale read versions, conflicting file claims, clashing delegated scopes, and concurrent worktree task claims. - Distinct S1 units: separate child runsubagent query loops under one parent, each with its own context, task and abort token; additionally, independently executed coord worktree task sessions in the explicit CLI mode. - Inter-S1 disturbance: child and parent edits can target the same file; WriteStore serializes writes, checks content versions and returns stale/conflict outcomes, while optional coord.Planner denies claims intersecting active file scope leases. - Attenuating coordination relation: root and child write tools share a per-workspace WriteStore with per-file serialization and read-base hash validation; explicit coord tasks additionally acquire scope leases before a compare-and-swap claim. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: tool failures, current-version conflicts and child outcomes return into model-visible tool results. A child can re-read and retry; the parent can repartition the remaining work after observing its child handoff. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: independently deciding child S1s can submit conflicting edits, and the shared write and lease checks reject those collisions with concrete state feedback. A simple spawn list or notification bus alone would not demonstrate this mutual interference attenuation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model specifies division of work and child scopes and can revise work after observed outcomes; child models respond to rejected changes. Deterministic guards arbitrate individual file/lease races, not the purpose of all task allocation. - Decision owner: model-backed parent/children for task-scoped reallocation and conflict response, with host-owned CAS/lease gates for safe execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTool.scope, shared WriteStore, child registry and result feedback, coord.Planner.claimnext scope lease followed by task CAS, WorkerPool and Reconciler. - Closure path: concurrent task assignment → child proposes workspace action → content hash/scope lease checks accept or return conflict → model obtains factual outcome and may retry/repartition; in coord mode tasks are reopened or merged with recorded status. - Boundary reachability: registered Agent and shared WriteStore are wired into server sessions, and coord run is a shipped explicit CLI path guarded by coord.workers (default enabled for that CLI entry only). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a shared execution mutex alone would be C; here independently deciding model workers and a model-facing delegation/result path supply an actual S1-to-S1 operational feedback loop. A default coord flag does not imply that all ordinary sessions enter the worktree planner. - Evidence: agenttool.py; writestore.py; coord/planner.py; tests/coord/testorchestration.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: scope protection concerns first-party write tools, not arbitrary subprocess file mutation, and coord&#39;s automatic claim order is deterministic rather than a free-standing model planner.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: maintain present control of the parent-and-child coding organization by assigning work, interpreting returned child outcomes, and changing descendant commitments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: child failures or cancellations, incomplete or obsolete subtasks, changed scope, exhausted child resources, stalled child action and user revisions during an active session. - Whole-system current view: parent receives each delegated child&#39;s terminal status, scoped file results and summarized handoff in its model context; server session endpoints expose the same current parent-session child roster, per-child sidechain and inbox/cancel status to the authorized operator. - Current-control decision scope: parent model chooses child tasks, bounded write scopes and follow-on tasks in response to a current cohort&#39;s reports; the human can inspect, abort and queue follow-up for a specific live child through the first-party server route. - Decisive decision or feedback right: assign and scope child work through the model-visible Agent tool, inspect returned child conclusions and decide follow-on allocations; in the distinct parent mode, inspect individual child transcripts and target running agents with cancel/follow-up/retry. - Decision owner: main model for model-mediated task allocation and later response; legitimate human session operator for the server&#39;s direct team-control endpoints (P). The deterministic tool dispatcher and UI cards do not themselves determine the purpose of allocations. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentTool task description/scope/role, distinct child result messages, liveagents status and targeted abort/inbox, sidechain metadata, server /v1/sessions/{sessionid}/agents control routes. - Closure path: parent model sends child commitments → independent children execute/report → parent model incorporates reports into new assignments, or human inspects a live sidechain and issues a targeted command → first-party runtime applies it and exposes updated child state. - Boundary reachability: SessionPool wires AgentTool into ordinary server sessions; supported session routes list child status, provide transcript and implement actual per-child cancel and follow-up. Ended-child follow-up persists pending instructions for retry. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model decides meaningful task allocation from current child feedback, not just fixed concurrency limits. Human mode actually changes a live child process rather than merely displaying historical cards. - Evidence: agenttool.py; subagentrunner.py; routers/sessions.py; tests/testmultiagentp2agentops.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: the main model generally resumes after its synchronous Agent call returns, not during an active child tool call; interactive running-child commands shown here are human-owned, queued follow-ups are consumed at settle, and MAXDEPTH=1 excludes an unlimited agent hierarchy.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent in-product assessment of an implementer&#39;s success claim with a distinct evidence path and enforced correction return was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child/model could claim a task is complete without correct code; the repository includes syntactic and lifecycle observations, but none is shown as an independent task-completion audit controller. - Claim being audited: prospective child final conclusions or merged task-worktree results; neither undergoes a first-party independently assigned reviewer check in the actually composed main session or coord run path. - Ordinary reporting path: child conclusion and structured ToolResult, progress/memory handoff, worker submission and reconciliation status. - Complementary access path: runtimeverification.verifyruntime can separately detect unmatched tool events, pending journal actions and missing cwd, but the query engine records it after the loop as a nonblocking diagnostic. coord.Reviewer.reject validates caller-provided findings but is not invoked by the live coord-run loop to generate those findings. - Independence boundary: diagnostics consult some underlying runtime facts, but the report cannot override the model result. A separately named reviewer class without a first-party invoking and adjudicating actor is not an actual independent audit loop. - Who acts on findings: an external caller could choose to pass findings to Reviewer; no automatic implementer-claim → independent judgment → changed completion/correction feedback loop is wired at this ref. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independently exercised right to deny a claimed successful response on semantic or prescribed evidential grounds in the shipping path. - Decision owner: none established; user-supplied findings or developer tests are outside this agent organization&#39;s independently deciding complement. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: passive runtimeverification, coord.Reviewer.reject, required structured findings and scope-subset enforcement, WriteStore incremental syntax checks. - Closure path: diagnostics → stored report without changed model answer; external findings → optional standalone Reviewer.reject can reopen a task. Neither closes a first-party independent audit path in active operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: testing basic syntax, enforcing version freshness, or checking a provided finding is execution integrity/coordination, not a sufficiently independent complementary claim challenge. - Evidence: runtimeverification.py; queryengine.py; coord/reviewer.py; cli/coordcmd.py. - Basis: explicit + bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: external integrators could supply an independent reviewer and wire it to Reviewer.reject; this is a possible composition, not observed first-party S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party long-horizon external scan, future capability choice, and strategic change returned to the present XEYO organization is shown. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/model information, plugins and memory can improve current work, but do not instantiate an organizational outside/future regulator. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct prospective first-party strategy revision right identified. - Decision owner: user/project maintainers configure future models and features; individual coding model chooses task-local tools and research. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web search and memory tools, session compaction and model choice, optional coord DAG and development G0–G6 stages. - Closure path: model can use external facts to solve the current task; no demonstrated diagnosis of changing future organizational environment → adaptation option → binding S3 change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prospective product development outside the running agent and present-task research are not the same as operational S4. - Evidence: python/ARCHITECTURE.md; python/coord/planner.py; python/engine/queryloop.py. - Basis: bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: a model can formulate a plan within a software project; this is task planning unless it demonstrably changes the organization&#39;s future capability allocation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no independent top-level mission/identity settlement with binding feedback into the runtime was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool permissions, user intervention, child scope, model instructions and fixed resource budgets constrain actions but do not create an organization-level policy/identity adjudication issue. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no in-system final policy authority over the organization&#39;s purpose is represented. - Decision owner: users set their tasks, approvals and follow-ups; developers define default capabilities and rules. Neither is shown as a distinct in-runtime constitutional feedback function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: permission gates, agenttype prompts, hard MAXDEPTH, write scopes, user cancellation, workspace-level settings and developer instructions. - Closure path: operation requested → permission/limit allows or rejects individual tool use; no ultimate-identity dispute → legitimate judgment → revised governing policy return path exists. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: operating constraints and legitimate human authority over an assigned coding task do not prove an ongoing S5 identity-setting institution. - Evidence: python/engine/agentroles.py; python/permissions/writescope.py; python/tools/agenttool/agenttool.py. - Basis: bounded structural absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: neither the user&#39;s absolute ability to stop a session nor repo-owner contributor policies may be silently promoted to operational S5=P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bbfbb3843aa0fd4058bbb956c2605fbb0971f5ff/assessments/xe/xeyo.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bbfbb3843aa0fd4058bbb956c2605fbb0971f5ff\">bbfbb38 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T20:52:46+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/boadij/pi-herdsman\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Pi Herdsman</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/boadij/pi-herdsman/tree/2b78e1b7b4a62240f7be881ab066be4cefbee612\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2b78e1b7b4a62240f7be881ab066be4cefbee612\">2b78e1b &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-owned completion of bounded coding/analysis work by directly delegated Agents and, in project mode, independently reasoning managed Leads. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing task requirements, file content, tool outcomes, partial work, failures and follow-up questions within one owned assignment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the current Lead/Agent model selects task decomposition, investigation, edits/tests, requests to its direct owner and final reported result; a project Lead retains implementation/validation judgment rather than having every step prescribed by Manager. - Decision owner: supported Pi model actors in the exact owned Lead/Agent sessions, with a Manager model choosing project assignment objectives when that mode is active. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent definitions, scoped tool allowlists, Herdr-launched Pi sessions, exact-session assignment records, required-evidence snapshots, durable request/result mailboxes, bounded context retirement and direct-owner result delivery. - Closure path: user/Manager objective → model-decided Lead/Agent tools and delegation → local task actions/evidence → completed session result or blocking question → exact owner receives result/answers and changes subsequent work or acceptance. - Boundary reachability: installed extension exports and registers delegateagent/continueagent and associated owner tools, starts managed Pi sessions, and injects active role policies. The advertised normal Lead and Manager modes reach the same real agent runtime; fixtures are not needed to supply the actor. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-owned Pi session leaves session/permission/transport machinery but not the task-specific choices or final substantive work. Provider mechanics are external; the first-party harness directly assembles and governs their installed task loops. - Evidence: delegation contract, agent controller, managed session runtime, project Lead charter. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for shipped wiring and local decision-right separation; no live provider task was attempted. - Caveats: tool execution and model inference depend on installed Pi/Herdr and selected provider; neither an external agent&#39;s unrelated internal metasystem nor user-defined agents are automatically credited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: project-level attenuation of interfering concurrent Lead assignments on branches/worktrees, with model-controlled corrective routing and worker-directed return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple S1 writers to one checkout, duplicate branch ownership, stale assignment/checkout identity, incompatible integration bases and cross-Lead conflicting changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Manager model chooses branch-scoped delegated objectives and correction/integration order, can steer the exact responsible Lead to reconcile an outdated base, and may revise assignments in response to report/conflict evidence. - Decision owner: Manager Pi model for substantive branch/assignment coordination; deterministic Herdsman/Git/Herdr checks enforce unique worktree occupancy and exact branch/session authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent branch-keyed ProjectAssignment, verified Herdr worktree group, assignment locks, peer eligibility and exact-session checks, nonterminal Lead results, messagestaff, guarded local fast-forward and needsupdate feedback. - Closure path: Manager delegates separate branches → each Lead works in its verified checkout → real collision/merge/availability evidence returns in staff/result/tool outcomes → Manager redirects an affected Lead, chooses integration order or pauses/resumes → subsequent S1 work and write placement change. - Boundary reachability: optional but shipped /manager mode registers delegatework, messagestaff, resumework, stoplead and integratebranch; a new project can create its first linked worktree, so this is not merely a hypothetical configurable API. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: branch placement, correction and conflict-handling judgment can be chosen by the Manager model; a lock or Git fast-forward alone has only deterministic authority and would not choose the task-level adjustment. - Distinct S1 units: at least two independently assigned Pi Lead sessions, each operating one branch/worktree and its owned Agent tree in the same verified project. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping writers could clobber a shared worktree or a later feature branch could be stale after earlier integration; an occupied checkout/duplicate assignment is explicitly rejected and needsupdate reports stale target state. - Attenuating coordination relation: Manager-selected separate branch ownership plus first-party exact occupancy/assignment checks, result messaging and corrective Lead direction. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: rejected ownership/new target SHA and Lead reports return to Manager, who uses messagestaff/resumework to assign the responsible Lead an updated base or correction before another accepted merge. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the branch/worktree anti-collision rules regulate a concrete interference among simultaneously active S1 writers, not merely pass messages or sequence arbitrary tasks. - Evidence: project orchestration, coordination role/assignment contract, registered staff tools, lead runtime. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported Manager mode. - Caveats: an ordinary single Lead has no inter-Lead S2 claim. Preventive worktree isolation is strong structural evidence but not proof of zero logical merge conflicts; the Manager model&#39;s decisions are distinct from validation enforcement.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current project-wide regulation of assigned Leads&#39; commitments, work lifecycle, intervention and integration by the model-operated Manager. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled or failed branch work, competing current assignments, incomplete required evidence, branch readiness, unavailable workers, and the order in which accepted branch outcomes enter the main worktree. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Manager model assesses project status and Lead reports, delegates/redirects, chooses whether to pause/resume a branch, accepts a specific validated source SHA and invokes local integration or requests more work. - Decision owner: active Manager Pi model for discretionary whole-project commitment/intervention choices; Chief is a separate optional higher-level model supervisor and cannot seize these branch rights. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: liststaff direct-report and work ledger, inspectstaff/readstafftranscript, event-driven completed-result/failure handoffs, persistent assignment records, messagestaff, stoplead, resumework, Git fast-forward/cleanup checks, optional human removal approval. - Closure path: current per-branch execution/status/evidence → Manager whole-project model judgment → instruction to exact Lead, stop/resume, revised delegation, or accepted-SHA integration → later Lead execution and verified project checkout/assignment state change. - Boundary reachability: normal /manager activation from the primary worktree injects a Manager role charter and a live Pi model with registered staff/project tools; no development-only controller or hypothetical future dashboard is needed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Manager owns priority, commitment and acceptance choices based on whole-project status; deterministic locks/Git/Herdr execute only selected calls. Chief&#39;s distinct direct-report scope is not evidence of duplicate authority at this project recursion. - Whole-system current view: liststaff projects exact current assigned/supervised Leads and branch work with active/paused/conflict status; inspect/transcript tools provide bounded additional evidence, while nonterminal reports/failures are automatically delivered to Manager. - Current-control decision scope: delegatework, corrective messagestaff, stoplead/resumework, accepted-SHA integratebranch and limited assignment required-evidence control; not merely a token budget, dashboard, process scheduler or close button. - Evidence: staff tool contract, project guide, Manager charter/registered tools, lead runtime. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for supported Manager mode and documented returned intervention. - Caveats: Manager integration is local Git only, never automatic hosted PR merge; its decision may be human-guided. Ordinary human permission to remove a checkout is a narrower destructive-action gate, not independently established project-wide parent S3 mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no sufficiently independent, first-party complementary operational-claim audit with corrective verdict authority is established at the declared project recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a Lead or Agent may assert completion after an incomplete implementation, missed tests or unsound branch evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an individual Lead validates its own technical result; Manager may inspect reports or require evidence and choose acceptance, but a separate evidence-gathering auditor is not an installed mandatory or demonstrably independently owned control path. - Decision owner: ordinary technical reviewer choices remain with Lead/Manager models and operator; no distinct first-party S3 owner is evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-Agent reports, reusable result references, required evidence, bounded transcript inspection, test/check actions and accepted-source-SHA integration guard. - Closure path: reports/evidence feed ordinary owner/Manager coordination, without a qualifying independent audit result and corrective-veto return before acceptance. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: reviewer Agent definitions inside the same Lead herd are explicitly internal; manager inspection and an exact SHA check are not equivalent to independent complementary operational audit. - Evidence: coordination review boundary, handoffs and accepted result contract, staff inspection. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium; a custom deployment could impose independent review, which is not credited at this standard boundary. - Caveats: no live provider-run audit was attempted; the negative does not forbid external independent reviewers.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no installed external-and-prospective capability-adaptation loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing Pi/Herdr capabilities, project context, new code requirements and agent-definition preferences. - Decisive decision or feedback right: current user/Manager/Lead can revise task plans or manually configure agent definitions; no system-owned outside/future adaptation authority is evidenced. - Decision owner: current-task models and operator, not a separate prospective intelligence function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent-definition discovery/overrides, role profiles, context handoffs, future session restarts, peer discovery and project status. - Closure path: configuration and internal task updates affect present execution, without an environmental forecast/options judgment returned into an S3 capability decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: learning task context, retaining transcripts or revising an active assignment does not itself model relevant future environmental change and adapt system capability. - Evidence: agent definitions, extension configuration, recovery guidance, coordination. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high for standard distribution. - Caveats: a user could compose an outside-oriented research Agent using generic task tools, but such external future adaptation is not a shipped system-level S4 path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level dispute/decision loop closed by an in-boundary agent or parent arrangement was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: exact-session authority, developer-set agent allowlists, project trust, sensitive checkout removal and branch permissions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: normal user grants/denies specific destructive action or changes configuration; runtime authenticates roles and applies exact-session ownership, not ultimate identity/ethos deliberation. - Decision owner: operator for ordinary permission/configuration; implemented session/role contract for enforcement; no first-party ultimate-policy decision maker demonstrated. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Manager/Chief/Lead role charters, scoped tool allowlists, session identity/lease validation, removal-preapproval UI, project trust and immutable assignment ownership. - Closure path: permission or configuration inputs gate individual operations and subsequent tool access, without an identity-policy issue referred to legitimate ultimate authority and returned to govern an agent organization. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: role names and a human approval over worktree deletion are not S5; technical authentication/authorization preserves safety but does not resolve system purpose or ultimate policy. - Evidence: role contracts and tool boundaries, project removal authority, coordination identity. - Basis: bounded structural absence. - Confidence: medium-high for standard distribution. - Caveats: a real user or institution may govern the project externally; its ultimate-policy arrangement is not silently inherited by this local Pi extension.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b2abcac5c588ebd4bd31f0e608ca33f8c6d0ae16/assessments/pi/pi-herdsman.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b2abcac5c588ebd4bd31f0e608ca33f8c6d0ae16\">b2abcac &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-28T21:26:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yohanvillarp/metamorph\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Metamorph</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-28\">2026-10-28</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/yohanvillarp/metamorph/tree/8871b937a4e6d0f6f5f9f08b4ca1e801fd98196c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8871b937a4e6d0f6f5f9f08b4ca1e801fd98196c\">8871b93 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: model-owned semantic transformation of individual migration files and bounded repair of failed build/source code in the active non-mock migration mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: varied framework conventions, imports/exports, code structure, tool results, API mismatch and incomplete or erroneous transformed files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a temporary Worker model decides source-specific edits via read/write/rename/create/delete/list tools, and a conditional integration-repair model chooses source fixes against real build errors; deterministic Mapper/PackageManager do not make these semantic model choices. - Decision owner: actual per-file Mozaik model agents created by first-party production InferencePort, and optional temporary model repair agent, under CLI-selected source/target framework and workspace limits. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: framework/architecture catalogs, shadow-root-bound tools, Mozaik inference adapter, configured model ID, Worker queue, file completion/status events, operator-selected source/target and deterministic task bookkeeping. - Closure path: FILEDISCOVERED and migration context → temporary model agent reads neighboring files and changes shadow source → actual file readback and inference completion → FILEMIGRATED → reviewer/build feedback or completed file outcome changes subsequent correction/work. - Boundary reachability: normal CLI run resolves non-mock credentials/config, passes shadow tools into MigrationRunner and production MozaikInferenceAdapter, then Worker enqueues startWorkerLoop, which creates and starts real runLoop models. --mock is a separate advertised simulation mode, not evidence of actual model judgment. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model from Worker leaves the file enumeration, package edits and event queue but not adaptive semantic rewriting; dynamic source/API choices are made by an inference-loop actor. Mock transformations are deterministic and are not borrowed as independent proof of autonomy. - Evidence: active Worker, Worker inference and file readback, per-file prompt/evidence, production Mozaik adapter, factory mock boundary. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: high for installed model-owned S1 wiring, medium for unexecuted live provider success. - Caveats: model provider and Mozaik runtime are external dependencies; --mock does not prove live model behavior. Predefined package/framework rules and deterministic file scans should not be confused with LLM-owned decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: model-judged attenuation of cross-file interface conflicts between concurrently migrated S1 file workers, with correction returned to the implicated Worker task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: one worker changing a component/export while another worker uses stale prop/import names, incompatible router screens, missing modules and inconsistent dependency structure across independently transformed files. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the separate Reviewer model examines the migrated file against neighboring imported modules, decides APPROVED/REJECTED/FATALMISMATCH, and sends concrete cross-file mismatch errors; the affected Worker model chooses the subsequent fix from the new evidence. - Decision owner: first-party separately launched Reviewer inference model for substantive interface-consistency judgment, followed by the responsible Worker model for correction. Deterministic syntax checks, concurrency caps and ready flags only transport/enforce. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: concurrency-limited Worker queue, global file-tree and neighbor/import context, original file readback, separate reviewer prompt/model, FILEREJECTED requeue with bounded retries and integration&#39;s failed-build repair feedback. - Closure path: different S1 file migrations create incompatible interfaces in one shadow project → Reviewer directly inspects updated source plus neighboring import/prop/export evidence → emits targeted rejection → Worker receives exact mismatch/neighbor hints and edits source → subsequent review/build checks the corrected relation. - Boundary reachability: actual non-mock run initializes both Worker and enabled-by-default Reviewer descriptors; per-file completions emit FILEMIGRATED to the Reviewer, whose independently created model then returns FILEREJECTED and Worker retries. Disabling Reviewer removes this agent-owned relation and invokes an explicitly different deterministic bypass. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the cross-file interface judgment is made by a Reviewer model from concrete code evidence, not merely by message routing or fixed phase guards; Worker model interprets correction within its source task. - Distinct S1 units: independently instantiated per-file Mozaik Worker inference sessions, potentially active up to configured concurrency, each transforming a file/surrounding source in a shared shadow migration. - Inter-S1 disturbance: one S1 changes a module&#39;s prop/export or file placement while a different S1 updates its consumer based on old assumptions; the resulting imports/route integration conflict across their outputs. - Attenuating coordination relation: first-party cross-file consistency reviewer model with project/neighbor evidence and explicit targeted reject/retry into the affected file worker. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: FILEREJECTED errors are embedded in buildFixRejectedPrompt, with actual disk/import evidence and a revised correction task before the next worker iteration. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the Reviewer prompt explicitly checks inter-file imports, public callback/export compatibility and shared router composition; the correction regulates incompatible co-produced S1 changes, not mere existence of an event bus or three-slot queue. - Evidence: Worker concurrency/repair path, project-wide file hints, cross-file review prompt and return, separate Reviewer participant, neighbor context. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for source-defined cross-file rejection loop; no live multi-file provider-backed migration executed. - Caveats: the Reviewer uses a context snapshot assembled by first-party code rather than arbitrary live file tools. If disabled or in deterministic mock mode the specific autonomous S2 witness is not exercised; locks/queues alone are not credited S2.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: first-party whole-migration-current plan/phase control through status aggregation, integration admission, deterministic cross-task remediation and terminal success/failure authority; autonomous discretionary project controller must still be composed. - Disturbance / variety regulated: pending/failed file and package tasks, lost completion signals, incomplete builds, malformed architectural output, excessive retries, stalled migration phases and uncertainty over whether the entire run is actually ready. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a function-specific coordinator/plan path exposes phase-start, project-wide work-status, requeue, retry-budget, migration success/failure and integration controls. Shipped policy makes those choices deterministically from task status and build/validator results; no agent chooses discretionary whole-project priority, resource reassignment or acceptance criteria. - Decision owner: currently deterministic first-party CoordinatorAgent/IntegrationAgent handlers and fixed configuration; a developer would have to compose an autonomous S3 controller with legitimate discretion over the exposed plan/current-control path to receive A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SQLite MigrationPlan, global pending/failed/completed records, planReadyForIntegration, event-triggered Coordinator, watchdog, integration locks, build/verifier diagnostics, requeued file tasks and dashboard reporting. - Closure path: all file/package task states plus integration build/validator evidence → current-plan coordinator/integrator determines phase transition, retry/repair set or failure/success within configured limits → PHASEINTEGRATIONSTARTED, FILEREJECTED or MIGRATIONCOMPLETED changes subsequent task execution and run state; discretionary model control is not wired. - Boundary reachability: ordinary CLI initialization joins the shipped Coordinator and Integration participants, invokes StateRepository plan methods, and exports createCoordinatorAgent, resolveRuntime and application agents as function-specific construction surfaces. These are active, not merely tests; the missing component is model ownership of the whole-current supervisory decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: source explicitly says the Coordinator does not call a model; the Integration temporary model performs local source repair after deterministic build failure, not project-wide current priority/commitment choice. Purely mechanical timeouts, phase gates and build pass/fail cannot be scored A. - Whole-system current view: the run-level persisted plan gathers every file task, package-manager task, phase, result and integration-round count; the Coordinator watches readiness and the UI/CLI can show progress, unlike one worker&#39;s local task report. - Current-control decision scope: dispatch integration only after all relevant work settles, reopen implicated file tasks, stop after retry/watchdog budget, and finalize a successful verified shadow migration; a future agent owner could use these direct controls to decide current task/resource commitments. - Evidence: model-free Coordinator and watchdog, integration control/repair/terminalization, ready-for-integration predicate, state/runtime API, CLI plan and progress. - Basis: structural function-specific constructor, without autonomous S3 owner. - Confidence: medium for positive construction path and high that present Coordinator is deterministic. - Caveats: C is not a claim that the built-in Coordinator autonomously manages a project. The optional dashboard is telemetry, integration repair model is task-local, and manual apply is a separate operator publication action—not a second parent-governed S3 mode.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: separate model-and-structural inspection of transformed source against migration intent and neighboring code, with binding rejection/correction feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker self-report mismatch, syntactically invalid transforms, hollow/incorrect UI moves, mismatched callbacks/exports, missing files and architecture drift. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independent Reviewer inference model judges file content and neighbor/canonical migration evidence with APPROVED/REJECTED/FATALMISMATCH; a rejection is acted upon through the built-in retry path before project integration. - Decision owner: a separately instantiated Reviewer model, distinct from the writing Worker model and its source-change loop; deterministic syntax/structure verifiers are complementary enforcement, not an autonomous decision maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: actual migrated file reread, built neighbor context, migration catalogs, syntax/structure validation, separate model role and optional reviewerModel, strict structured JSON result, status/event persistence, bounded repair queue and integration verification. - Closure path: a Worker announces a migrated file → first-party code rereads the real shadow source and checks syntax/structure, independent Reviewer model analyzes direct file and neighbor evidence → approval completes task or reject/fatal feeds the Worker/plan → corrected file is rechecked before whole-project build and eventual application. - Boundary reachability: the default live registry joins Reviewer (separate from Worker) and FILEMIGRATED activates model inference in the normal CLI. --disable-agents reviewer intentionally selects a different zero-token bypass mode and removes model audit, but does not invalidate the shipped default autonomous path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Reviewer model, not Worker, owns substantive correctness/architecture verdict; fixed syntax checks alone would not justify A, and mock/disabled-reviewer auto-approval must not be mistaken for independent audit. - Claim being audited: a model Worker changed one source file in the shadow workspace correctly for the selected framework, with valid imported APIs and intact application structure. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker inference completion and FILEMIGRATED, plus file content readback and task status, supply the standard implementation report. - Complementary access path: independent Reviewer gets a first-party filesystem-derived copy of the migrated code, neighbor module context and migration rules; syntax/structure checks also inspect current disk evidence independent of the Worker model&#39;s final text. - Independence boundary: Reviewer is a separately instantiated model participant with its own review instruction and structured verdict, no worker editing tools; the same configured provider/model may serve both roles, and Reviewer does not have arbitrary direct tool browsing beyond supplied snapshots. - Who acts on findings: built-in FILEREJECTED/FILEFATALMISMATCH handlers requeue the affected Worker or mark it failed; the whole-migration Coordinator/Integration then waits, validates or fails rather than treating a worker&#39;s self-attestation as success. - Evidence: live Reviewer/bypass distinction, independent reviewer model/structured verdict, reviewer direct-evidence prompt and return, retry return, pre-inference structural checks. - Basis: explicit and structural. - Confidence: medium-high for source-defined independent review return; no live LLM run. - Caveats: reviewer independence is a separate model context and directly sourced artifact snapshots, not necessarily a separate vendor or direct unbounded file access. Deterministic bypass mode auto-approves and would not itself meet S3.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped process autonomously senses external/future migration-market distinctions, generates long-horizon capability options, selects adaptation and returns that choice into present Metamorph capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: emerging framework changes, future migrations and platform developments are addressed by release catalog maintenance rather than a first-party outside-and-then intelligence system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no model or legitimately delegated parent owns a documented prospective external/environmental adaptation decision inside the running migration organization. - Decision owner: none established at this boundary; the user picks from currently supported framework pairs, and maintainers may later ship new rules as adjacent development work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static layered migration catalogs, runtime framework detection, hints for current Next.js migration structure, model selection/configuration, telemetry and release documentation. - Closure path: no prospective sensing → new strategic migration capability option → authoritative adaptation choice → returned changed agent/config/capability cycle. Target selection and rule-based current-source transformations are S1/planning input, not S4. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model Workers infer code changes inside one declared migration objective, but do not determine which future environmental opportunities should change Metamorph&#39;s own capabilities. - Evidence: current migration catalog, current Next migration hints, CLI target selection, static catalog architecture. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: medium-high for current production paths. - Caveats: OSS maintainers can add migration catalogs or change provider models, but this external development control is not a first-party operational S4 return loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no shipped ultimate-identity/constitutional policy adjudication with authoritative model or parent decision and return into subsequent runtime governance. - Disturbance / variety regulated: whether to publish a migration, which framework to target, credentials and resource limits are handled by users or fixed configuration, not identity-level disputes resolved by a Metamorph policy institution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator selects migration goal and later invokes apply; CLI checks successful run and prevents double application. Those are acceptance and safety boundaries, not policy-identity creation or arbitration. - Decision owner: no qualifying autonomous or parent S5 function owner in this system. The user can set goals and permissions, but there is no first-party identity-dispute return path distinct from current migration acceptance. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shadow isolation, apply success gate/branch, config precedence, reviewer/model disabling rules, explicit rollback and user-owned repository/credentials. - Closure path: completed shadow migration → user chooses apply or rollback → target branch publication changes a particular project; no identity-level issue → legitimate constitutional authority → binding charter decision → return into future operating model. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: users and maintainers establish fixed scope/configuration outside the run; model workers cannot amend Metamorph&#39;s purpose, ultimate-policy rules or the user&#39;s repository authority. - Evidence: manual apply gate, migration integrator, runtime plan/config, CLI settings, reviewer bypass mode. - Basis: bounded first-party absence. - Confidence: high for absence of an explicit ultimate-policy loop in the examined runtime. - Caveats: contributor governance and apply permission are legitimate human acts, not function-specific S5 closure; the reviewer is an audit actor, not an identity authority.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5320f4ba71cf1e427ab339a0f4d9cc9f1c802d0c/assessments/me/metamorph.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5320f4ba71cf1e427ab339a0f4d9cc9f1c802d0c\">5320f4b &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-24T18:46:47+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TheAppliedScientist/TheAppliedScientist\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TheAppliedScientist</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-24\">2026-09-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/TheAppliedScientist/TheAppliedScientist/tree/762824fd41598370e75588861b48991b0a9fd784\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 762824fd41598370e75588861b48991b0a9fd784\">762824f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform the scientific work that directly produces the system&#39;s intended outcome — literature-informed experiments, analyses and manuscript revisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete or weak evidence in the starting paper, experimental/setup failures, alternative baselines/datasets/metrics, observed results, code/runtime errors, manuscript gaps and corrective review feedback. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which concrete experiments, analyses, code changes and manuscript edits to perform in order to improve the current research submission. - Decision owner: the autonomous Scientist model actor executed by the first-party Scientist runner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validated paper-task JSON, isolated Harbor/native workspace, container/host process execution, artifact synchronization, compile/version scripts, timeout/resource configuration, Search API transport and submission persistence. - Closure path: task/paper/code plus current evidence and any prior reviewer feedback → Scientist chooses and performs literature/experiment/manuscript actions → execution/results/artifacts return to the Scientist → Scientist updates the working research artifact and proceeds to review or another operational iteration. - Boundary reachability: ./tas run is the documented standard entrypoint; it stages the Scientist instructions and workspace and invokes the configured model-driven Scientist directly in both Docker and native modes. No benchmark or paper-evaluation actor is needed for ordinary S1 reachability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the runner constrains environment, timeout and persistence, but it does not choose the substantive experiment or revision. Removing the Scientist actor while retaining scripts/services removes the research decision right. - Evidence: README.md; components/ai-scientist/run.sh; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user inputs establish the paper/task boundary and external model endpoints supply the actor implementation; S1 autonomy is scoped to first-party orchestration of the Scientist within those constraints.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 relation is established at the reviewed job boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the standard job exposes one primary Scientist operational unit. Search is an information service and Reviewer is the complementary-audit path; background experiment processes do not by themselves establish distinct autonomous S1 units with an interaction-generated oscillation/conflict. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: background experiment execution, service endpoints, job/workspace isolation, artifact syncing, timeouts and sequential review/version transitions. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: component/process plurality transports or supports one research operation and its metasystemic audit/intelligence functions. No reviewed path makes a discretionary coordination response to a specific inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: README.md; components/ai-scientist/run.sh; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional experiment subprocesses and separately deployable services are implementation topology, not evidence of peer S1 coordination.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no autonomous whole-system current-control function is established at the reviewed job boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the runner tracks one job, timeout, environment, resume artifacts and service endpoints, while the Scientist manages task-local research work; no separate actor is shown regulating current shared commitments/resources/priorities across operations on behalf of the whole system. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S3. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: run.sh timeout/environment/resource setup, task validation, workspace creation, artifact sync, resume state, process/service health and deterministic submission/version sequencing. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Scientist makes operational research choices and the Reviewer makes complementary audit judgments. Deterministic runner/service machinery enforces prior configuration but does not own discretionary whole-system current-control decisions. - Evidence: components/ai-scientist/run.sh; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md; components/ai-scientist/scripts/submitforreview.sh. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: reviewer rejection can force corrective work, but that relation audits the produced research artifact and is mapped under S3, not whole-system resource/commitment control.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge the Scientist&#39;s current manuscript/research claims through a separately deployed fresh reviewer with its own evidence-gathering path, then return actionable findings into another scientific revision and re-review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported or overstated claims, missing/obsolete related work, weak experimental design/baselines, methodological confounds, framing/presentation defects and other research weaknesses that can survive the producing Scientist&#39;s ordinary execution and self-inspection. - Decisive decision or feedback right: produce the substantive peer-review judgment over the current submission — strengths/weaknesses, concrete requested improvements, questions, methodology/positioning critique, scores and accept/reject recommendation. - Decision owner: the autonomous Reviewer model actor executed inside the first-party AI Reviewer service; in the documented main loop this actor is reached through REVIEWERMODE=api-external, separate from the Scientist&#39;s driving session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate Reviewer HTTP service, fresh temporary review task/trajectory, review-specific prompt, Search CLI/literature index, async submit/poll protocol, review extraction, persisted reviewercommunications/response.md, version snapshots and deterministic submission/version bookkeeping. - Closure path: Scientist produces current experiments/manuscript → submitforreview.sh sends the current LaTeX to the separate Reviewer API → a fresh Reviewer trajectory reads the paper and gathers independent literature/methodology evidence → Reviewer returns weaknesses/actionable suggestions/judgment → response is frozen into the submission and read by the Scientist → Scientist performs experiments/analysis/manuscript corrections and writes a rebuttal → Scientist resubmits → the Reviewer service runs a fresh review over the revised manuscript; the loop repeats until questions are satisfactorily addressed. - Boundary reachability: the documented all-components setup installs/starts Reviewer and Scientist together; run.sh defaults to REVIEWERMODE=api-external and localhost Reviewer endpoint; the Scientist&#39;s shipped instructions say this external-review path is mandatory for the main loop and explicitly prohibit replacing it with subagent/ensemble review. The positive path is therefore part of the supported standard distribution rather than an adjacent evaluation harness. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Reviewer actor makes the semantic audit judgment; HTTP polling, versioning and persistence only transport/record it. Under the counterfactual owner test, retaining those mechanisms without the Reviewer leaves no equivalent independent assessment of the manuscript. - Claim being audited: that the current research manuscript and its represented experimental contribution are sufficiently sound, novel, supported and positioned for scientific submission. - Ordinary reporting path: Scientist-produced experiment code/results/figures and the manuscript generated from its own research loop. - Complementary access path: a separate Reviewer service receives the materialized current manuscript, launches a new review trajectory, independently searches the literature and re-reads exact paper evidence under a review-specific procedure. - Independence boundary: the default main loop uses a separately deployed API reviewer rather than the Scientist session; each API job constructs a new temporary review task/trajectory from the current LaTeX, and the review procedure has its own search/evidence rules. Previous review history is not needed to make the new audit judgment. - Who acts on findings: the Scientist actor, which is instructed to address reviewer weaknesses/questions through new experiments, analysis, literature work and manuscript edits before resubmission. - Evidence: README.md; components/ai-scientist/run.sh; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md; components/ai-scientist/scripts/submitforreview.sh; components/ai-reviewer/reviewapi.py; components/ai-reviewer/prompts/paperreviewerinstructiontemplate.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: optional subagent/ensemble implementation paths exist in the submission script, but the documented Scientist main-loop configuration explicitly selects the separate api-external reviewer. S3 credit is based on that supported mode and its corrective/re-review closure, not on generic reviewer vocabulary or the accompanying paper&#39;s performance claims.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: interrogate the external scientific environment and develop prospective research/revision options that alter the current experiment and manuscript trajectory. - Disturbance / variety regulated: relevant prior work, missing citations/positioning, external baselines and methods, newly identified research gaps, and uncertainty about which additional experiments or analyses would best strengthen the future submission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose literature investigations and, from the resulting external evidence, choose prospective experiments, analyses or framing changes to incorporate into the next research state. - Decision owner: the autonomous Scientist model actor operating under the first-party research process; the separate Reviewer can additionally surface external gaps through its own literature search, but S4 credit does not depend on treating the Reviewer judgment as S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party Search API/CLI over the scientific corpus, downloaded literature/notes, paper task context, mutable experiment/manuscript workspace and deterministic scripts for execution/compilation/versioning. - Closure path: current paper/results expose an unresolved research/positioning need → Scientist searches and reads external scientific literature → external distinctions reveal related work/gaps/baselines/options → Scientist chooses a prospective experiment/analysis/framing change → performs that work in the current workspace → new evidence/manuscript state becomes present operational capability and is available to the next review/revision cycle. - Boundary reachability: the shipped Scientist instructions make literature review and repeated /app/search use part of the standard research process, including revisiting literature throughout the run; top-level documented tasks explicitly direct the Scientist to use Search to find important gaps and run evidence-backed improvements. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Search provides external observations, but the Scientist chooses queries, interprets scientific distinctions and selects what future experiment/revision option to execute. Removing the Scientist while retaining Search leaves no equivalent prospective adaptation choice. - External distinction: literature/related-work/baseline/method evidence returned from the Search service and full papers. - Future / prospective distinction: which additional experiment, analysis, citation/positioning change or research correction should be attempted in the next version rather than merely describing current state. - Adaptation option generated: a concrete evidence-backed experiment/analysis/manuscript-positioning change for the next research iteration. - Path back into current capability / S3: the selected option is executed by the Scientist in the mutable experiment/manuscript workspace, changing the present research artifact and evidence set before the next submission. This closure exists even though no separate autonomous S3 owner is established at the reviewed boundary. - Evidence: README.md; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md; components/ai-reviewer/prompts/paperreviewerinstructiontemplate.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic experiment iteration and review-driven correction are not the basis for S4. The credited function is specifically the externally oriented literature/environment inquiry that develops prospective scientific options and returns them into subsequent research capability.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the reviewed job boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the user-supplied Task, paper/code/reviews, WhatNOTToDo, provider/model choices, timeouts and static research-quality rules constrain the run, but no identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue is routed to a legitimate authority and returned as a governing decision. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: validated task JSON, static Scientist/reviewer instructions, provider configuration, timeout/resource settings, safety/security guidance and submission quality requirements. - Closure path: not applicable for the negative finding. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Scientist can alter experiments and manuscript content but is not given authority to redefine the system&#39;s identity or ultimate policy; the Reviewer owns quality challenge, not constitutional authority. Human task definition before a run is ordinary external task-setting, not a demonstrated first-party S5 parent loop. - Evidence: README.md; components/ai-scientist/run.sh; components/ai-scientist/.claude/CLAUDE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: operator/provider configuration bounds autonomy but does not by itself establish S5 or P.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3fd8349d2730787470ef7c24c3eb8ef08b080bba/assessments/appliedscientist.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3fd8349d2730787470ef7c24c3eb8ef08b080bba\">3fd8349 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T17:19:58+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/listepo/cox\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">cox</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-24\">2026-10-24</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/listepo/cox/tree/d4eb49f1f283f95d233785c452e3be8ea28cb5eb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision d4eb49f1f283f95d233785c452e3be8ea28cb5eb\">d4eb49f &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: respond autonomously to changing code and shell/web evidence while producing user-requested project outcomes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing information, source changes, test failures, conflicting implementation constraints, task-specific uncertainty and tool errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose tools, file changes, investigative steps, new child tasks and follow-up actions based on actual observations. - Decision owner: main model-backed Session; delegated explore/shell child Sessions separately own bounded model/tool decisions for assigned contributions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: protocol Event stream, provider adapters, tool traits, sandbox, permission engine, context archiving and budget/failure gates. - Closure path: user/parent task → model request → model tool decision → first-party execution → tool result/feedback → further model task action or answer. - Boundary reachability: main sessions install the agent tool, built-in workspace tools and provider-neutral loop; children create separate Sessions and run real model requests as shipped behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects and revises substantive operations; deterministic event and permission machinery cannot decide the code changes. - Evidence: turn engine, session construction, child dispatch. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: no live effectiveness measurement; bundled default child presets are narrower than user-defined agents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate inter-worker source mutation clashes through an explicitly selected separate writable worktree for each independently acting child S1. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel shell/coding workers sharing a mutable checkout can overwrite or invalidate each other&#39;s workspace effects; parent-to-child directions also must not corrupt the child&#39;s running turn. - Distinct S1 units: the main coding Session and separately model-driven agent child sessions; multiple children can run concurrently with their own delegated tasks, histories and tool actions. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent writable shell/custom worker operations can change common project files and invalidate another&#39;s assumptions or overwrite the main agent&#39;s edits. - Attenuating coordination relation: the parent model can choose agent(isolation: &quot;worktree&quot;) per worker to separate mutation domains and stop shared checkout effects. Parent-to-child follow-ups are queued to turn boundaries. The implemented sibling relay has causal-hop and reception limits but built-in child presets do not expose sendmessage to children; no default child-to-child messaging topology is claimed. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolated child executes in its own Git worktree; completion names the branch for parent integration, and messages become the addressee&#39;s next model turn (including resumed completed children), so later actions can adjust based on the coordination decision. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: model-selected isolation addresses the specific shared-checkout interference of concurrently acting child workers; message delivery is supporting current-task feedback, not credited as independent S2 merely because messages can be routed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select whether each agent task requires isolated mutation space and what conflict-resolution instruction to send a particular running/finished child. - Decision owner: parent model for optional isolation and task-specific follow-up decisions; fixed protocol queue/cap behavior belongs to the developer/runtime, not to the model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git worktree creation/owner locks, deterministic task registry and relay, message delivery caps for optional custom-agent modes, atomic child slot reservations. - Closure path: parallel task planning/concurrent effect risk → parent chooses isolation and/or directed follow-up → runtime creates isolated branch/queues one child turn → child adapts actions on its actual workspace/instructions → returned branch/response informs subsequent parent/worker decisions. - Boundary reachability: agent is installed into standard Sessions; its advertised isolation input calls the shipped worktree interface configured by CLI, while core sendmessage/task registry supports parent and child messaging in the normal loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the parent model explicitly selects a function-specific effect-domain option and message contents for live worker conflict management; deterministic locks and flood caps merely enforce selected and fixed constraints. - Evidence: AgentTool options and dispatch, task relay/guards, worktree implementation, normal-session worktree wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: worktree isolation is opt-in, not default; no proof that agents always choose it or merge correctly. Built-in explore/shell child presets do not grant sibling sendmessage, so optional custom-agent relay capabilities are not evidence for this S2 state.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: govern the active team of independently progressing worker commitments under a shared parent task and budget by allocating bounded jobs and redirecting ongoing assignments. - Disturbance / variety regulated: oversubscribed child budgets/capacity, stalled or no-longer-useful subtasks, changing parent priorities, delayed responses and an active portfolio whose work must still support one goal. - Whole-system current view: parent tracks child IDs/names and running/finished state via the task registry and receives TaskCreated/TaskCompleted, usage/result notices and child-to-parent messages; the TUI /tasks and /agents views expose the active set to its operator. - Current-control decision scope: the parent model chooses which children to create, the tasks/presets, relative spend slices and isolation and can direct running or finished child sessions with new instructions; fixed concurrency and permission rules enforce bounds. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select/revise concurrent child commitments in light of existing job responses, cost and new overarching task requirements. - Decision owner: the main cox model, as current manager of its spawned S1 worker team. Operator can steer and approve specific operations, but no separate parent-governed S3 portfolio mode is claimed. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child task registry, shared budget accounting, atomic maxconcurrentsubagents reservations, per-child cost slices, background task notices, sendmessage relay and user-visible task status. - Closure path: whole-team known task IDs/progress and changing objective → parent model allocates bounded jobs or sends revised task direction → runtime spawns, resumes or queues the targeted child → child does new work and returns cost/results to the parent. - Boundary reachability: AgentTool is registered in main sessions, task ownership and message delivery are wired through ordinary runtime, and child progress is returned to the same main model context; no user-script manager or development-only orchestration is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting and revising commitments across independently model-backed children requires parent model judgment; slot guards and cost accounting cannot choose which job matters next. - Evidence: Session agent registration and task identity, AgentTool budget/child creation, task status and parent feedback, UI current task view. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: this is a bounded current child-team view, not cross-repository enterprise governance; no empirical demonstration of quality or optimal budget reallocation is claimed. Generic decomposition alone would not suffice without the active feedback/resumption/revision path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no shipped independently controlled review judgment using complementary direct operational evidence and binding corrective feedback is instantiated. - Disturbance / variety regulated: routine test failures, build output, tool permission issues and correctness claims can be checked by the same operational agent; that alone is not distinct audit. - Claim being audited: whether coded changes meet requested quality/correctness beyond the coding actor&#39;s own report. - Ordinary reporting path: shell/test tool results and child output/rollout summaries return to parent coding context. - Complementary access path: read-only explore child may inspect files, but is a generic delegable investigator; tests and sample named reviewer presets are not shipped with a required independent reviewer judgment and corrective return. - Independence boundary: a separately instantiated evidence-bearing review agent with authority to challenge operational claims is not wired by default; optional user-authored agent definitions cannot be assumed to exist. - Who acts on findings: parent model/operator reacts to tool or child findings as normal coding decisions, not mandatory or independent S3 control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct audit judgment owner established, although any authorized coding/test process may report diagnostics. - Decision owner: operational model and configured tools, not a separate first-party audit actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: shell/test tools, archived outputs, evals/ and user-configurable child presets. - Closure path: tool/test report → coding model action exists; independent audit probe → separate judgment → returned S3 correction is not instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent-directed code review can be composed by users, but shipped generic exploration and same-task test outcomes do not confer independent complementary audit ownership. - Evidence: presets and dynamic definitions, tool-output workflow, eval harness. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: an operator could explicitly create a reviewer subagent, but that would be a different composed operating mode needing its own evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped autonomous externally and prospectively oriented capability-option search and adoption return to present organizational operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current task web context, provider/model suitability and accumulated session facts are handled for immediate execution or remembered context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no distinct decision over future harness capabilities based on horizon scanning and tested alternatives. - Decision owner: operator/developers choose available models, plugins, future roadmap work and package settings; the current model selects task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tier router, model metadata, optional plugin advice, webfetch, end-of-session memory extraction and config changes. - Closure path: current task information → tool/tier/model decisions and retained facts exists; future environmental model → adaptation option → adopted current capability is not instantiated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: budget-aware tier selection and extracted task facts are not prospectively changing the organization&#39;s capability or policy. - Evidence: model route advice, memory extraction, project README. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: development plans and external advisory plugins could produce strategy elsewhere, but are outside the reviewed shipped decision closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime institution owns final identity/ultimate-policy decisions and returns them into later operation at cox&#39;s assessed session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: risky tool calls, spending limits and access to secret/out-of-workspace paths are handled by fixed policies and operator approvals, not a durable ethos/identity deliberation loop. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: system role and mission are configured by shipped prompts and user task; no in-product authority resolves a dispute about the harness&#39;s ultimate purpose. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: no positive S5 mode; user/developer sets permissions, model tiers, configuration and source behavior. - Return-to-operation path: permission denial/approval changes concrete tool execution; identity-level question → legitimate ultimate parent decision → binding return is not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: granular tool-approval and configuration decisions, not ultimate policy/identity adjudication. - Decision owner: operator for per-tool authorization and developer for product defaults; neither closes a first-party identity-level parent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: coxpermission engine, sandbox, redaction and plugin capability restrictions. - Closure path: tool risk classification → allow/ask/deny → tool outcome; no identifiable S5 identity controversy and authoritative return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent executes under fixed/user policies, while legitimate identity decisions are not delegated or procedurally fed back at this recursion. - Evidence: permission workflow, runtime tool safety, configuration. - Basis: structural bounded absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: upstream OSS governance and commercial licensing are different organizations/recursion levels and are excluded.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c1f7f0e2be892f551007e5cddfa6212c8bbb3ad0/assessments/co/cox.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c1f7f0e2be892f551007e5cddfa6212c8bbb3ad0\">c1f7f0e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-01T14:48:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nurmdrafi/pi-native\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">pi-native</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-01\">2026-11-01</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nurmdrafi/pi-native/tree/b4e99cf3d2f60a1fd5e810bdfcbcbf47b3692e8f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision b4e99cf3d2f60a1fd5e810bdfcbcbf47b3692e8f\">b4e99cf &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: task-directed engineering transformations by deployed worker cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown code, errors and changing task conditions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose reads, edits, commands and follow-up actions to satisfy a task brief. - Decision owner: the installed top-level/worker agent; the host supplies model execution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: native tool wrappers, sandbox loadouts, tmux lifecycle, result delivery and session history. - Closure path: tool observations inform later agent turns; final result and git deltas return to the parent. - Boundary reachability: bootstrap links Pi and installs the local extensions; subagent tool launches installed worker role with native tools and returns its result. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing agent choice leaves process/tool execution machinery but no discretionary engineering transformation. - Evidence: scripts/bootstrap.sh; extensions/subagents/index.ts; agents/worker.md; extensions/piedit/index.ts. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: requires installed Pi, model access, tmux and built native binaries; no ownership borrowed from other Pi features.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuation of write collisions and shared-provider saturation across distinct child work cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two writable children target the same path/subtree; concurrent requests can generate provider 429 storms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: expose declared write scopes and conflict feedback; autonomous conflict-specific arbitration beyond the configured queue requires composition. - Decision owner: developer-selected queue/cap policy plus agent-supplied file scope declarations; no separate shipped autonomous arbitration policy credited. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: findConflicts, queued/running scope reservations, FIFO skip-scan and bounded provider concurrency. - Closure path: result finalization releases a scope/slot and drain launches the waiting child; acknowledgements and list output expose ownership to the parent. - Boundary reachability: the installed subagent tool consumes files/cwd, checks reservations and parks conflicting spawns in the default queue mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: S2-specific collision paths exist, but deterministic waiting alone does not establish agent ownership of the regulatory decision. - Evidence: extensions/subagents/scope.ts; extensions/subagents/spawn-queue.ts; extensions/subagents/index.ts; docs/subagents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: undeclared write scopes are not locked; worktree-isolated absolute paths do not conflict; queue enforcement is not an autonomy claim. - Distinct S1 units: separately executing writable worker children. - Inter-S1 disturbance: overlapping file/subtree modification and shared-provider quota contention. - Attenuating coordination relation: declared-scope conflict detector plus reservations and concurrency cap. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a blocked child starts only after owner result/lock release; parent receives overlap acknowledgement and later results. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the launch condition depends on concrete competing ownership of the same path or provider quota, rather than task sequence alone.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: current whole-session regulation of child commitments and intervention on behalf of the engineering task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stalled, duplicative, expensive or no-longer-useful live work across the child set. - Decisive decision or feedback right: cancel, restart or steer a selected running/queued child after observing current session-wide state. - Decision owner: the parent coding agent in subagent-enabled mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: subagentslist exposes phase, elapsed/turns/tokens/cost and scopes; control interrupts/kills and frees locks/slots. - Closure path: chosen intervention changes running/queued work; exactly-once finalization returns outcome as a steer message and frees resources for subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: installed tool registration exposes list, message and control to the parent; those handlers reach live child registry/cancel/restart functions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the agent chooses which current commitment to change; deterministic budget stops, alone, would enforce only preselected ceilings. - Evidence: extensions/subagents/index.ts; extensions/subagents/status.ts; docs/subagents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: bounded to one session&#39;s child set; not a claim of organization-wide governance or a distinct human parent mode. - Whole-system current view: all this session&#39;s registered/running/queued children with live metrics and scope ownership. - Current-control decision scope: stop or re-dispatch current child commitments, steer work and thereby release/reallocate occupied scope and execution capacity.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optional complementary source inspection that can challenge a worker&#39;s completion/correctness claim. - Disturbance / variety regulated: worker summaries or normal test reports omit defects, races or guard regressions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently judge the actual diff and surrounding code; return APPROVE or REQUESTCHANGES with reproducible findings. - Decision owner: the installed read-only reviewer agent; parent agent receives and acts on findings. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: separate role context/tool loadout, direct git/piread/test access, durable review report and ordinary subagent result delivery. - Closure path: review findings are delivered to the parent as a new steer turn; the parent can revise work or dispatch correction before committing. - Boundary reachability: reviewer is a shipped named role discoverable by subagent registration, with isolated context and direct artifact inspection; delivery wakes the existing parent session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: audit judgment comes from a distinct actor with complementary raw-artifact access, not the worker&#39;s summary or a verdict parser. - Evidence: agents/reviewer.md; extensions/subagents/index.ts; extensions/subagents/README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional source-first review of a handed-off change, not automatic coverage of every claim; routine worker tests and the verifier&#39;s naming alone are not credited. - Claim being audited: worker&#39;s changed code is correct and safe to commit. - Ordinary reporting path: worker final summary and git-delta result. - Complementary access path: reviewer independently obtains git status/diff, reads callers/surrounding source and runs narrow checks. - Independence boundary: isolated child context; read-only role cannot implement its own fix or silently reuse the worker&#39;s narrative as evidence. - Who acts on findings: the parent engineering agent, through editing or corrective worker dispatch after delivered review.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no outside-and-then capability-adaptation loop established at the deployed boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/tool/environment change would require prospective sensing, options and return into capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped decisive prospective adaptation right established. - Decision owner: not established; operator/maintainer updates are outside this mapping. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: researcher, docs-optimizer, history and startup self-heal can support tasks or maintenance. - Closure path: no evidenced prospective option-to-current-capability loop in the installed standard modes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: web research for an assigned task, history injection and path/version repair do not themselves decide future harness capability. - Evidence: extensions/self-heal/index.ts; agents/researcher.md; agents/docs-optimizer.md; docs/skills.md; docs/subagents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: does not rule out user-authored research/optimization work; repository benchmark/dogfood is excluded.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity or ultimate-policy dispute/decision closure established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: conflict about purpose or ultimate risk policy, rather than individual edit/command permission. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party identity-level authoritative resolution path found. - Decision owner: operator owns initial configuration; no qualifying runtime S5 parent loop evidenced. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SYSTEM.md, role restrictions, permission gate and settings constrain operation. - Closure path: ordinary deny/note/approval and config updates regulate actions but do not close identity-level deliberation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing an agent leaves static rules; neither enforcement nor generic operator involvement establishes ultimate-policy ownership. - Evidence: SYSTEM.md; settings.json; extensions/permission-gate/index.ts; extensions/permission-gate/rules/phase.ts; docs/subagents.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: maintainer contribution/release governance is adjacent to the installed product.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/bea54f8cb9ea92ca56b7054a30242db9b6ef9298/assessments/pi/pi-native.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit bea54f8cb9ea92ca56b7054a30242db9b6ef9298\">bea54f8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-02T20:55:11+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Anon4You/Laudacode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Laudacode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-02\">2026-10-02</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Anon4You/Laudacode/tree/f8906ce9da3b11c83fca4b296e0a90f5c7d39110\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f8906ce9da3b11c83fca4b296e0a90f5c7d39110\">f8906ce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform repository-facing software-engineering work by interpreting the task, inspecting code/environment state, selecting coding tools, applying changes or running commands, observing outcomes and revising subsequent actions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous repositories, ambiguous requirements, file/git state, command/test/LSP failures, provider/network errors, tool denials, changing workspace state and implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what evidence to inspect, which allowed coding/tool action to execute, what edits/commands to attempt, whether to delegate independent chunks, how to respond to returned evidence, and when to finish. - Decision owner: the model-backed Laudacode main agent; delegated specialists instantiate the same operational decision pattern for their assigned work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: first-party tool registry; approvals/permissions; retry/backoff; token/cost budgets; post-edit hooks; managed processes; MCP/LSP adapters; undo/checkpoints; session persistence. - Closure path: task + project context → model chooses a tool/action → Laudacode authorizes and executes it → environment/tool result returns into model context → the model chooses the next coding action or final response. - Boundary reachability: ordinary laudacode and laudacode exec paths instantiate the shipped loop directly; no downstream application-authored orchestration is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: without the model-backed actor, Laudacode retains tools and enforcement but no open-ended task-specific coding decision owner. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent.rs; src/tools.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: external provider inference is a dependency; the assessment credits Laudacode&#39;s first-party role/tool/feedback composition, not provider internals.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent delegated specialists can share one workspace, including mutating specialists, but no concrete first-party interference relation is paired with an attenuation mechanism in the delegate runtime. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S2. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: the main model is told to delegate independent chunks; a delegate call caps fan-out at four; role toolsets constrain capabilities; main-agent writes are sequential within its own tool loop. - Closure path: not applicable; no standard path was found from an identified interaction/oscillation among distinct delegated S1 units through an attenuation relation and back into their subsequent behavior. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: selecting ostensibly independent tasks and gathering reports is delegation/fan-out, not a demonstrated S2-specific regulation of mutual interference. - Evidence: src/agents.rs; src/agent.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the model may avoid collisions by task design, but Methodology 0.3.6 requires the concrete inter-S1 disturbance and attenuation/feedback relation rather than generic decomposition.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system current-control loop was established above the delegated S1 population. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the main agent can choose a delegate batch and later receive reports, but it does not receive live worker status/commitment/resource state or intervene in an in-flight batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at S3 level. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-selected specialist/tasks; maximum four delegates; blocking joinall; aggregate token/cost accounting; global cancellation/approval; managed process controls. - Closure path: not applicable; the main agent launches a batch and regains control after fan-in, rather than receiving a live whole-system current view and exercising current resource/priority/commitment decisions over active S1s. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: pre-dispatch delegation and post-completion replanning remain S1/orchestration behavior without the required current-control function. - Evidence: src/agents.rs; src/agent.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the main model can start a later wave after reports arrive, but no live current-management surface over the in-flight worker population was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently challenge implementation quality/correctness and return audit findings to the main coding actor for corrective action. - Disturbance / variety regulated: bugs, unsafe assumptions, style/edge-case defects, missing verification and test/build failures that an implementing S1 may overlook. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a separately instantiated reviewer/tester specialist makes the audit judgment over workspace evidence; the main agent decides subsequent corrective work using that returned judgment. - Decision owner: the fresh model-backed reviewer and/or tester specialist invoked through the main model&#39;s delegate tool. - Claim being audited: that the implementation/current code is correct and sufficiently verified after coding. - Ordinary reporting path: the implementing main/coder S1 reports its own tool outcomes and completion through its coding conversation. - Complementary access path: delegate can launch a fresh read-only reviewer over current files and a separate tester with build/test command access; each runs its own conversation and restricted tool loop. - Independence boundary: reviewer/tester agents have fresh message histories, role-specific system prompts and restricted tool sets, separate from the implementing S1&#39;s conversation; the reviewer is structurally read-only. - Who acts on findings: executedelegate returns specialist reports as the main agent&#39;s tool result; the main model then receives those findings in its ordinary loop and can edit, test, or delegate corrective work. - Boundary reachability: delegate, built-in reviewer/tester roles and the independent sub-agent loop are exposed by the standard first-party agent tool surface; the main system prompt explicitly recommends “reviewer + tester after coding”. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: specialist tool allowlists; read-only reviewer flag; separate MAXSUBROUNDS; shared permission boundary; concurrent delegate execution; post-edit hooks as additional deterministic evidence. - Closure path: implementation exists → main model delegates review/test → fresh reviewer/tester directly inspect/run evidence → reports return as the delegate tool result → main model receives findings and chooses corrective work or accepts clean results. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic hooks can add evidence, but the credited complementary judgment is made by a distinct model-backed specialist. Removing that specialist leaves only self-checks/hooks and removes the independent semantic audit judgment. - Evidence: src/agents.rs; src/agent.rs; src/repl.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the user-facing /review path by itself surfaces a report to the UI rather than automatically feeding it into the main agent; the positive closure relies on the model-invoked delegate reviewer/tester path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: web/MCP research, session history, conversation compaction, skills, provider/model selection and persisted project notes can inform present work but do not operationalize future environmental intelligence into persistent organizational adaptation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for prospective capability/strategy adaptation. - Decision owner: none established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: websearch/fetchurl; skills; sessions/checkpoints; compaction; custom commands; provider/model configuration; AGENTS.md project instructions. - Closure path: no external/future distinction → adaptation option → persistent capability/strategy change → later operational return loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: current-task research and durable context can improve present execution without constituting S4. - Evidence: README.md; src/agent.rs; src/session.rs. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: AGENTS.md is credited under S5 for durable project policy, not S4; persistence alone is not prospective adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: A(P) - Function: establish and preserve the durable project-level standing instructions that define how later Laudacode coding work understands the project, its build/test expectations, code layout and conventions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: missing/stale project guidance, inconsistent future-agent assumptions, loss of durable project conventions and divergence in standing build/test/working expectations across sessions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide the semantic content of the project AGENTS.md layer that later Laudacode agent construction loads into the system prompt. - Decision owner: Base (A) mode — the model-backed main agent invoked by /init inspects repository evidence and chooses the synthesized project overview, commands, layout and conventions. Parent (P) mode — the legitimate project owner can directly review/edit the same authoritative file or append standing project instructions through #note. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what durable project-level rules, conventions and working expectations should govern later Laudacode coding behavior at this project recursion. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Base (A) — the model-backed /init run owns semantic synthesis of the initial AGENTS.md within existing higher-level Laudacode/user constraints; Parent (P) — the legitimate project owner/editor owns direct changes to that same project-instruction layer. - Return-to-operation path: AGENTS.md persists in the project; subsequent Agent::buildsystemprompt calls load it and inject its content into the main system prompt before later coding turns, changing the standing instructions under which the agent operates. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: repository exploration through ordinary read/search tools; model-authored file write during /init; direct owner edits/#note; persistent filesystem; loadagentsmd in system-prompt construction. - Closure path: project instruction gap/change → model-driven /init synthesis or legitimate owner edit → AGENTS.md persists → later Laudacode construction loads the file into system prompt → subsequent coding work is governed by the returned standing layer. - Boundary reachability: /init and #note are shipped first-party user surfaces; /init directly invokes the standard main agent loop, and the same shipped Agent::buildsystemprompt path loads AGENTS.md for later ordinary sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: invoking /init is a trigger, but the model decides the exact project-rule content from repository evidence; removing the model leaves only the offline stub and external editing. In parent mode the project owner directly chooses the authoritative edit. - Evidence: src/repl.rs; src/agent.rs; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this is project-recursion S5. It does not claim Laudacode&#39;s model can rewrite higher-level application safety rules, permission semantics or user authority.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4df216e5e3c2796d349b9a80c9334ddbd9baf720/assessments/laudacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4df216e5e3c2796d349b9a80c9334ddbd9baf720\">4df216e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-15T04:50:35+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pedapudi/foe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Foe</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-15\">2026-10-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/pedapudi/foe/tree/167455955b79ee31e7e8ac3c2ecfff40a6b14ec2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 167455955b79ee31e7e8ac3c2ecfff40a6b14ec2\">1674559 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute one bounded code investigation/edit/test/report task through model-guided tool use and environmental feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: different source tree/test observations, errors, model/tool outputs, tool denials, returned messages and task-specific constraints. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what source/tool/action to inspect or modify next and when to report a solution, blockage or need for assistance. - Decision owner: the model-driven episode actor, inside the first-party Foe autonomous run loop; externally hosted inference computes responses but does not supply a separate harness&#39;s execution/control organization. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Rust tool registry and coding functions, sandbox/grants, logged model turns, budget, completion verifier and typed outcome. - Closure path: objective/inbox → model-request → proposed tool call → first-party validated dispatch → result recorded in log → next model observation/action or terminal verified outcome. - Boundary reachability: standard foe executable with built-in coding/oneshot/team contracts launches this first-party loop without borrowing an external coding-agent CLI, eval fixture or user-authored work scheduler. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model chooses task-specific actions and reacts to feedback; deterministic constraints veto unsafe/over-budget work but cannot choose the semantic next solution step by themselves. - Evidence: README.md; crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/cli/src/run.rs; crates/code/src/lib.rs; docs/design.md. - Basis: explicit shipped wiring and code. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deliberately model-less deterministic workflow alone would not justify S1=A; positive evidence is actual model/tool episode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate work interference and oscillation between distinct concurrently operating child episode S1s in a team, especially contested mutable code paths and task dependency cycles/overlap. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two S1s trying to modify overlapping filesystem scopes, stale dependent work launched too early, messages/questions that would induce duplicate or conflicting efforts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select independent/non-overlapping work partitions, keep shared surfaces with the lead, order dependent units, and revise subordinate work when child results or risks change. This is a specific model-directed coordination judgment; the kernel&#39;s overlap refusal is the enforcement layer. - Decision owner: the first-party model-driven team lead exercising spawn, blockedby, send, steer, cancel and wait in the shipped builtin:team contract; the deterministic board denies invalid overlapping roots and prevents concurrent writers. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: canonical path overlap check on board admission and at child launch, dependency DAG, unique task assignment, single-writer log projection, per-child grants/sandbox, peer-message delivery with reply ids/deadlines. - Closure path: leader partitions tasks → Team::delegate rejects an overlapping current write root unless dependent → scheduler admits disjoint ready children with restricted grants → child outcomes and notifications return through durable team log/board/inbox → leader&#39;s next model turn revises/continues partition, or reorders/cancels affected work. - Boundary reachability: builtin:team provides this rule explicitly and provides model-callable coordination tools; separately launched child processes, scoped grants and board are part of the shipped CLI/runtime distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead&#39;s task-specific choice of conflict-free partition and follow-up from actual worker feedback is not determined by the static scheduler. Removing the lead&#39;s model leaves mechanical collision rejection/dependency order but removes the actual substantive team-level response to coding conflicts; this distinguishes owner from enforcer. - Distinct S1 units: separately running worker and surveyor episodes with their own model/tool loops or read-only task outcome, in the root team; recursive children may add subordinate teams. - Inter-S1 disturbance: potential simultaneous writes under the same source directory, incompatible edits to shared interfaces, dependent changes proceeding before prerequisites and worker waiting/communication stalling. - Attenuating coordination relation: leader-authored partition/ownership rules, immutable in-boundary child grants, overlap-refusal and dependency gating, bounded reply correlation and cancellation. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: board/task revisions and child outcome/inbox evidence influence leader&#39;s next spawn/steer/cancel/integration decisions, and those decisions return to affected child tasks. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: concurrent autonomous child S1s share writeable source resources, the code detects specific overlapping write conflict before launch, and leader-owned adaptive partition/replanning responds with enforced new S1 boundaries. This is concrete interference attenuation beyond mere channels or task-order fields. - Evidence: crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/team/src/libtest.rs; docs/design.md; crates/core/src/spawn.rs. - Basis: explicit causal code, supported built-in mode and structural owner test. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: A is limited to a model-driven lead in builtin team mode; kernel-only overlap enforcement is deterministic and would not separately be S2=A. Local quality/correctness of final merged code is not guaranteed by partitioning.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate whole-current commitments, bounded resources, team membership and intervention at the declared single-root-team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: insufficient child capacity or token/time budget, blocked dependencies, failed/cancelled workers, incomplete/unaccounted deliverables, unnecessary or wrong delegated units. - Decisive decision or feedback right: from a team-wide board/roster and returned worker outcomes, decide which unit to perform internally, which child to spawn and with what scope, which child to steer or cancel, and how/when to integrate accounted work or exit. - Decision owner: the model-driven lead episode in builtin:team. Team::schedule, budget pool, verification and log are bounded machinery executing decisions rather than owning team-level discretionary work/commitment allocation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: task board with queued/running/terminal revisions and owner/dependencies, roster, shared child budget reservations and releases, team/wait tools, bounded completion accounting, stop and recovery. - Closure path: leader reads the current team/task/worker state → chooses spawn/steer/cancel/wait/reallocation/integration → admission, execution and status changes are journaled and enforced → next model turn sees settlement/progress and can revise current commitments. - Boundary reachability: packaged built-in team lead and team tools operate in the normal binary without a separate coordinator service, external human dispatcher or development-only example. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the lead chooses which work and constraints to commit in light of current whole-team state; deterministic budget rules, task statuses, and verifier account checks only transport and enforce those choices. - Whole-system current view: team-wide log-projected task board (task id, owner, status, dependencies, write scopes, outcomes), roster, messages, and shared descendant budget accounting at this team&#39;s recursion, via first-party team, wait, and task-result surfaces. - Current-control decision scope: delegation, prioritization of current units, disjoint grants, current resource reservations (within declared ceilings), intervention/cancellation, task accounting and final integrated commitments; not ultimate organization-wide policy. - Evidence: crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/core/src/budget.rs; docs/design.md. - Basis: explicit shipped loop and agent-decided team regulation. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: S3 belongs to this root team&#39;s recursion, not Foe-wide stewardship across different user installations. No positive parent-governed variant inferred from an operator providing the initial task or inspecting the viewer.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no complementary independent organizational inspection and corrective-return relationship distinct from the normal root lead/episode feedback is established in supported runtime modes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incorrect coding changes, verifier errors, missing task accounting or unsafe execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: a complementary actor would independently inspect direct evidence about current operation, challenge ordinary S3 reports, form an audit finding and cause a corrective decision/return. - Decision owner: the configured verifier/tool produces determinate acceptance/findings as part of ordinary execution; team lead accounts for units and responds to returned findings; no separate independent S3 corrective authority is shipped. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: donewhen.verify and verifier result events, append-only logs, final outcome evidence, lead&#39;s wait/task accountability, sandbox/grants and optional viewer/benchmark reports. - Closure path: verifier reports can trigger same episode&#39;s retry or failure; child outcome reports can trigger lead response. No separate independently owned complementary audit path into S3 is demonstrated. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model tool feedback or externally authored deterministic verifier is ordinary quality control, and read-only surveyors are task contributors, not guaranteed independent evidence auditors with corrective return rights. - Evidence: crates/core/src/loop.rs; crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; docs/evidence.md; docs/design.md; docs/evaluation.md. - Basis: bounded source-negative mapping. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: a particular configured independent code audit episode can be constructed; no mandatory first-party S3 arrangement or dedicated audit correction path in standard modes is evidenced.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no shipped prospective environmental/capability adaptation loop returning from external future intelligence to the operational team&#39;s capability baseline. - Disturbance / variety regulated: evolving coding ecosystem, new threats/provider interfaces, accumulated failures and hypothetical future runtime improvements. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently select future capability/strategy changes based on outside/forward-looking evidence and return adaptation to currently executing coding organization. - Decision owner: human operators/maintainers choose upgrades and configuration; local model may propose coding changes as its task, but the standard runtime does not own autonomous future organizational adaptation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: generic coding/edit tools, optional task-specific research, log history, candidate workflows and trajectory evidence, manually selected self-improvement workflows. - Closure path: examples/self-extension and evals/terminalbench can assess evidence and suggest edits in isolated evaluation tasks, but are adjacent eval/demo workflows and do not automatically promote a runtime/capability change into ordinary deployed Foe operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: changing some code under a coding task is ordinary S1; neither task-specific model planning nor append-only learning artifacts autonomously chooses future whole-harness adaptation from external environment. - Evidence: docs/self-improvement.md; docs/deferred.md; docs/design.md; README.md. - Basis: explicit documented limitation and bounded absence. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: Foe&#39;s research reports describe bounded source-edit loops but explicitly say autonomous selection/promotion/general self-improvement remain unsupported; no inference that S4 is impossible to build atop its runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: the runtime enforces author-defined operational grants and contract values but no independently closed ultimate identity/policy governance function exists at selected team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsafe tools, write authority, budget and task scope, blocked questions and possible policy conflicts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide or legitimately escalate ultimate purpose/identity conflicts beyond current work, return binding resolved policy to ongoing operations. - Decision owner: human/operator authors outer contract and permissions; children are granted only narrower scoped contracts. Model lead cannot legitimately override the organization&#39;s ultimate source of authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: immutable contract fingerprints, grants and path overlap, permission/sandbox checks, role-defined child contracts, typed completion outcomes, agent ask and block. - Closure path: pre-authored rule → kernel enforcement. Bounded question/answer and ordinary task routing can return an operational answer, but no first-party ultimate identity-level dispute → legitimate authoritative adjudication → new organizational policy → subsequent governed operations path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: an immutable access boundary or model choosing to self-block is operational safety, not S5 ultimate values; operator ability to launch/update an episode is not an S5 parent-governance mode. - Evidence: crates/contract/src/document.rs; crates/core/src/grants.rs; crates/team/src/lib.rs; crates/cli/src/builtin-team.json; docs/design.md. - Basis: source-bounded absence. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: individual host institutions could implement identity/policy governance using the host protocol, but this is separate from the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5928c8889e1b4da97bb7f7c571c3b63c28758eec/assessments/foe.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5928c8889e1b4da97bb7f7c571c3b63c28758eec\">5928c88 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-24T00:23:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/context-labs/whip\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WhipCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-23\">2026-10-23</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/context-labs/whip/tree/801b3a4f9412356cf36934701bce67a1fe30f6bb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 801b3a4f9412356cf36934701bce67a1fe30f6bb\">801b3a4 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: independently choose, execute, inspect and revise substantive coding and repository actions in a bounded session or delegated child task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing code, instructions, tool errors, model responses, failed host operations, rejected permissions, children and new user messages. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the root/child model selects rlmexec cells, file/shell/agent calls, retry or alternative action from observed host output. - Decision owner: configured inference model in each first-party AgentSession; fixed host modules admit and enforce but do not choose the coding solution. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentSession.RunTurn, single rlmexec tool, isolated kernels, durable transcript/context handles, typed host modules, scoped permissions and goal-loop support. - Closure path: coding request/current context → model-chosen host operation → file/tool result, failure or denial → subsequent model operation or returned completion. - Boundary reachability: built-in Coding() is the default shipped definition; daemon binding gives both normal roots and spawned child sessions this model/tool loop, and production modes route through the same recursive runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: substantive operations vary with inference decisions, rather than being entirely predetermined by a script. - Evidence: agent-loop documentation, coding definition, agent session. - Basis: frozen production-source tracing and documented shipped operating model. - Confidence: high for operative construction, not live model performance. - Caveats: no model/provider endpoint or live Whip agent was run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate cross-child interference over shared work and coordinate changes to delegated task boundaries while children make independent operating decisions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two workers attempting incompatible shared-state writes, concurrent file edits, stale progress updates, overlapping responsibilities and changed dependent task results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the parent model chooses which independently deciding child tackles which files/tasks, what capabilities are delegated, and how to react to returned failures or shared blackboard conflict; children can reread a rejected CAS and revise their own update. - Decision owner: root or intermediate parent model for task partition/repartition; each child model for retry after observed conflict; daemon/SQLite owns atomic enforcement only. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agents.spawn/submit, one-hop durable mailbox, blackboard CAS/version and subscriptions, file mutation coordination locks, child budgets and delegated authority. - Closure path: concurrent child work reveals overlap, stale version or result dependency → shared-state CAS rejects a stale update and mail/subscriptions expose changed state → model rereads and can change its update, ownership partition or child instructions → revised S1 action. - Boundary reachability: Coding() installs agents, messages, state and file operations for real root and child kernels; shared-state CAS and subscriptions are wired into the daemon host. Direct-child messages and parent follow-up invoke retained production agents. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: atomic CAS and same-path locking alone would only be deterministic support; here an actual model has task-partition authority and can use failure/version feedback to alter subsequent independent child work. This is bounded to conflicts visible through the supplied coordination surfaces, not automatic semantic merging of arbitrary shell edits. - Evidence: concurrency ownership, host tools and CAS contract, shared-state store, boundary regression. - Basis: runtime source, shipped guidance and deterministic regression source inspection. - Confidence: medium-high for an optional model-owned conflict regulation path. - Caveats: the parent must actually choose suitable decomposition/reconciliation; the runtime does not independently make semantic cross-file conflict judgments. - Distinct S1 units: concurrent independently model-driven retained child AgentSession workers, with local prompts/transcripts and side-effect capabilities. - Inter-S1 disturbance: incompatible updates to blackboard/work files or reassignment of work after a sibling reports a result. - Attenuating coordination relation: atomic CAS and file locks refuse stale/shared mutations, while parent model-directed task partition and follow-up steering change who works on what. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: CAS error includes the current version; state subscriptions deliver updates; child completion/failure and custom messages wake the parent, which can steer children or change subsequent delegation. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: two simultaneous operational owners and a concrete stale-write/overlapping-task disturbance are identified; CAS refusal and subsequent agent-owned repartition/retry regulate that interference. Mere mail delivery is not credited independently.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P) - Function: supervise current commitments and constraints of the active root/child organization, selecting targeted intervention while work is already in progress. - Disturbance / variety regulated: stuck or unproductive children, resource oversubscription, wrong delegation, changing user priority and current permission exposure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: parent model chooses from agents.list/inspect results whether to submit revised work, stop/delete a subtree, change follow-on delegation or narrow capability/budget; in a separate human-client mode the operator can inspect the entire agent tree and cancel/stop/revoke. - Decision owner: the parent inference model in autonomous mode; the session&#39;s legitimate human operator in client-governed mode. Daemon actors only serialize and enforce the selected change. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: current agent statuses, budget accounting, agents.submit/stop/delete, subtree terminalization, shared notices; inspector tree, active-turn cancellation, grant revocation and protocol controls. - Closure path: currently observed child state/message/budget → model or human decision to steer, stop, cancel or revoke → daemon admits an authorized control → active/queued child work or effective authority changes → further state is observable. - Boundary reachability: Coding() installs current-control agents tools and production runtime implements list/inspect/submit/stop; the shipped packages/app inspector maps live snapshot permissions to concrete stop/cancel/revoke commands. Both modes are connected to the same session store, not test-only controllers. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-decided supervision is a higher current-cohort regulation loop beyond assigning one new task; the operator mode is legitimately parent-owned and separately closed. Fixed worker admission, kernel pools and quota checks alone are not the S3 owner. - Evidence: recursive runtime controls, protocol client control, agent inspector, budget and grant inspector. - Basis: source-traced model and first-party human-client ownership modes. - Confidence: high for current-control rights and return; actual quality of prioritization unmeasured. - Caveats: a root model can inspect/control its direct children, not bypass ancestry to arbitrary grandchild transcripts; every recursion is governed by its immediate parent. Operator intervention requires a connected authorized client. - Whole-system current view: model sees the active direct-child set and per-child status/budgets/messages at its recursion; operator tree view shows all retained descendants and active turns with effective controls. - Current-control decision scope: selective stop/delete, midturn steer, new queued input, child task redistribution, capability revocation and budget/permission constraints rather than only mechanically scheduled execution.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no installed first-party complementary audit function is established for claims or actual consequences of normal coding work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unsupported success or correctness claims could require a reviewer; the frozen runtime provides generic tools to create one, not a material audit-specific owner/return by default. - Decisive decision or feedback right: an ad hoc spawned child can be asked to review, but no first-party auditor-specific mode, independent inspection requirement, verdict contract or application of findings is packaged. - Decision owner: an external user/workflow designer would have to supply an actual auditor prompt, evidence scope and follow-up policy; Coding() and the daemon supply only general-purpose model/task primitives. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: optional agents.spawn with narrowed read/shell capabilities, models.batch independent completions, messages, transcripts, context handles, tests and eval verifier. - Closure path: a user-composed reviewer might inspect files and send a message, but neither a first-party claim-relative independent judgment nor the findings-to-correction circuit is bound as a supported audit-specific runtime path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple independent model calls and a child literally named reviewer are insufficient without function-specific audit ownership and complementary operational evidence access. This is a bounded no-path result, not an impossibility of composing a review externally. - Evidence: generic reviewer example, built-in coding definition, named reviewer test fixture, evaluation workflow. - Basis: first-party definition, actual exposed modules, documented composition and adjacent evaluation boundaries. - Confidence: medium for scoped negative; generic composability should not be confused with a first-party S3 constructor. - Caveats: a user-installed audit-specific custom definition could change another deployment&#39;s assessed boundary, but it is not in the frozen standard distribution.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no first-party future-facing external sensing, adaptation option judgment and return into operational capability was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new providers, MCP tools, coding conventions and later tasks could require future adaptation, but inspected mechanisms process current configuration and tasks. - Decisive decision or feedback right: model can inspect existing catalogued skills/tools and create present-task schedules; users can import existing skills/MCP configuration. No first-party prospective external-capability selection and closed adoption cycle was observed. - Decision owner: operator for available skill/provider integration and user instructions; task model for current coding choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: schedules module, project/standing instructions and skills discovery, MCP selection/import, persistent me.md notes, context compaction, model/provider choice, goals. - Closure path: current user/project configuration is loaded or a future timed task is queued; no evidenced outside/future options → evaluation → capability-change decision → return to present S1/S3 ability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: remembering task facts and loading existing instructions are not the strategic prospective adaptation function. - Evidence: runtime environment assembly, feature and skills contract, memory, coding definition. - Basis: installed-module and supported-configuration review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: users may design and install specialized future-scanning agents; this is external composition, not credited first-party closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no internal or formally returned parent decision loop over ultimate organizational identity, mission and governing policy is established within an installed coding session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: operations can raise safety/permission conflicts, but the handled issues concern authorizing particular tools and resources, not adjudicating the organization&#39;s ultimate purpose. - Decisive decision or feedback right: operator grants/denies current tool actions or revokes child authority; developers set fixed default policies; no first-party ultimate-identity dispute process and binding decision return is exposed. - Decision owner: user/developer for current permissions, project goals and configuration, without a separately instantiated S5 governance function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: recursive permission grants, capability issuer chains, modes, user.ask, kernel limits, provenance, instruction hierarchy, audit trail and operator control. - Closure path: requested side effect → permission grant/denial → operation permitted or blocked; this is tactical authorization, not identity/policy dispute → legitimate final authority → returned constitutional ruling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: strong deterministic boundaries and the human&#39;s ownership of a requested task do not automatically create ultimate organization-level S5. - Evidence: permission and authority runtime, concurrency/authority, client policy/control, definition/identity. - Basis: first-party trust boundary and control-plane source review. - Confidence: medium-high for bounded negative. - Caveats: a human can change the task and software settings, but this does not evidence a complete S5=P loop at the session recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cb2b50d4a237e6757b5e5e1875aac5069febe9d2/assessments/wh/whipcode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cb2b50d4a237e6757b5e5e1875aac5069febe9d2\">cb2b50d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T09:34:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wkbin/taixu\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TaiXu</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/wkbin/taixu/tree/037880a898ad503128dee7b2991dd6d351ad8cb3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 037880a898ad503128dee7b2991dd6d351ad8cb3\">037880a &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed work through persistent model/tool execution, including workspace, shell, browser/MCP and other first-party tools, and return tool observations to the model until the work concludes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing workspace state, tool outputs/failures, schema errors, loop conditions, external resources and task-specific follow-up needs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which available tool to call, with which task-specific arguments, and whether to continue using tools or conclude. - Decision owner: the configured main-session model actor; bounded child lanes independently own the same kind of local operational discretion for their assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HarnessLoop, provider protocol, HarnessToolRoundRunner, ToolExecutor, schema/loop checks, persisted session/lane transcript, tool budgets and approval gates. - Closure path: user request/history → model chooses tool call → host validates/executes → ToolResult is persisted into the conversation → later model round observes the result and chooses the next operational action or final answer. - Boundary reachability: this is TaiXu&#39;s standard shipped chat/agent runtime; subagent lanes use the same first-party provider/tool protocol inside the packaged distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model removes the task-specific choice of tools, arguments and stopping point; deterministic machinery constrains and executes those choices but does not replace their ordinary operational discretion. - Evidence: HarnessToolRoundRunner.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt, HarnessLoop.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: prevent destructive interference among concurrently executing subagent S1 units that share a workspace, especially overlapping writes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more child lanes can otherwise concurrently modify the same file/directory or whole workspace, causing write collisions and invalidating each other&#39;s operational results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the task-specific write lease for each delegated S1 ([], exact paths/directories, or []) and thereby determine which child operations may safely coexist. - Decision owner: the main-session model actor issuing invokesubagent; first-party prompt/schema makes writePaths part of the model&#39;s required task decomposition/coordination decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: buildWriteCleanWaves, global parallelism gate, per-lane execution-time write-scope rejection, normalized path checks, blocked-write accounting and structured batch summaries. - Closure path: main model defines multiple child tasks and write leases → orchestrator detects lease overlap and partitions work into non-conflicting waves → lane gate blocks any structured write outside the selected lease and returns the failure as a tool result → the child changes later behavior, while later conflicting waves wait → parent receives combined outcomes for further coordination. - Boundary reachability: invokesubagent is a first-party model-facing tool in the ordinary system prompt/runtime; the shipped guidance explicitly requires writePaths, and SubagentOrchestrator/SubagentLaneRunner enforce the same fields without downstream code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the host decides deterministic overlap/scheduling from the chosen leases, but the task-specific coordination right — what each child may write and whether it is read-only/scoped/exclusive — belongs to the main agent. Without that model decision the host does not infer materially equivalent task-specific leases. - Evidence: subagentguidance.md, SubagentArgsParser.kt, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, SubagentLaneContracts.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: the lease gate does not cover arbitrary shell writes through base; the shipped prompt states this boundary and directs strict-isolation writes through structured write/edit tools. - Distinct S1 units: separately named/resumable subagent lanes, each running its own bounded model/tool loop with its own operation/transcript while contributing to the parent job. - Inter-S1 disturbance: concurrent lane writes to overlapping workspace paths can collide or invalidate peer work. - Attenuating coordination relation: model-declared write leases plus deterministic conflict partitioning into write-clean waves and execution-time path enforcement. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: out-of-lease writes are returned to the offending child as failed tool results; conflicting tasks are delayed to later waves; blocked/incomplete outcomes are returned to the parent for repair rather than silently treated as complete. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited relation is explicitly designed around the concrete shared-workspace write-interference mode and changes when/how affected child operations may write.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: supervise the current set of child operations as a whole, allocate their task/model/write commitments, and intervene when a child is incomplete, blocked, awaiting approval, timed out or in conflict with the larger delivery. - Disturbance / variety regulated: partial/failed child work, blocked writes, pending approvals, timeout, model/role mismatch, conflicting returned conclusions, and changing current commitments across a multi-child batch. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the child portfolio and per-child task/role/model/write commitments, then use the returned whole-batch outcome to resume a lane by taskid, re-dispatch with changed leases, perform an approval/write itself, resolve conflicts or proceed to final delivery. - Decision owner: the main-session model actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: batch outcome aggregation, structured termination/approval/blocked-write status, persisted lane transcripts and resumable lane ids, concurrency gate, timeout/recovery machinery, parent system prompt and main-session tool loop. - Closure path: main model creates current child commitments → child lanes execute under S2 leases → orchestrator returns one ordered batch summary covering every child&#39;s result/status → the main model is explicitly instructed not to treat blocked/incomplete outcomes as complete and can revise current commitments through another tool round → subsequent child/main operation follows the revised decision. - Boundary reachability: all elements are wired into the ordinary first-party invokesubagent path and main chat loop; no development-only supervisor is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic concurrency/timeout machinery enforces limits, but the main model chooses the actual task portfolio, roles/models/write scopes and exception response. Removing it leaves no actor making the same task-specific whole-job interventions. - Evidence: subagentguidance.md, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, SubagentLaneRunner.kt, HarnessLoop.kt. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: simple single-agent runs need not exercise S3; the state is established by the standard multi-subagent mode. - Whole-system current view: the parent receives the ordered outcome set for the entire dispatched batch, including each task&#39;s success, termination, tool-call count, pending approvals, blocked writes, read-only/write-intent condition and resumable lane identity, alongside its own current session context. - Current-control decision scope: create/withhold child commitments, choose role/model and write authority, resume a specific lane, redispatch failed/blocked work, personally complete approval/write obligations, reconcile returned conflicts and decide whether the current job is ready to close.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a child S1&#39;s self-reported completion using a host-side evidence path that sees independently persisted operational receipts rather than trusting the child summary alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a child model can claim completion even though required verification commands never succeeded, claimed files were not actually written, or a criterion is manually unverifiable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine whether each declared acceptance criterion is backed by host-observed successful command/file-write receipts and downgrade unsupported complete to partial before the claim reaches parent control. - Decision owner: deterministic first-party host adjudication; no autonomous independent auditor owns the verdict at this ref, so the audit path is classified C rather than A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persisted lane transcript, extraction of successful ToolCall/ToolResult command and write receipts, criterion parser, downgrade-only adjudicator and parent-facing adjudication rendering. - Closure path: child emits optional completion claim → host independently reads persisted execution receipts → criteria are reconciled against those receipts → unsupported completion is downgraded → adjudicated result is included in the parent-facing subagent outcome → main S3 sees the corrected operational claim rather than the child&#39;s unsupported status. - Boundary reachability: claim parsing/adjudication is first-party runtime code used by SubagentOrchestrator on ordinary child results; it does not depend on tests or an external evaluator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the complementary evidence channel and corrective return are operationally present, but the decisive audit verdict is fixed host logic rather than an autonomous audit actor. The implementation therefore provides a function-specific constructor/audit path without autonomous S3 ownership. - Evidence: SubagentClaim.kt, SubagentOrchestrator.kt, persisted lane transcript in SubagentLaneRunner.kt/session storage. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: claims without a valid claim block fail open to legacy behavior; C reflects the available first-party audit path, not universal auditing of every child response. - Claim being audited: the child model&#39;s assertion that a delegated subtask is complete and its acceptance criteria have actually been satisfied. - Ordinary reporting path: the child lane&#39;s own textual conclusion/status returned through normal subagent summary. - Complementary access path: host-persisted successful tool execution receipts for commands and structured file writes, which are not controlled by the child claim text. - Independence boundary: the child produces the claim, while host runtime derives receipts from persisted execution events and applies downgrade-only rules; the host never upgrades an unsupported child claim. - Who acts on findings: the host modifies the status before returning it, and the main S3 receives the adjudicated result for follow-up/re-dispatch or final-delivery control.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: turn evidence from completed user-facing work into a prospective option to evolve TaiXu&#39;s reusable skill capability for future tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: repeated/reusable user workflows not covered by current skills, or operational experience revealing defects in an existing reusable skill&#39;s instructions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: accept or reject the proposed capability adaptation and, when accepted, create/update the persistent custom skill used by future operation. - Decision owner: the human operator through the first-party skill-suggestion card/apply action; the model-backed advisor develops the adaptation option but does not autonomously install it. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: post-run gating after successful tool-heavy work, conversation digest, current skill inventory, separate advisor model call, normalized create/update proposal, SkillSuggestion message/card, SkillSuggestionActions, and AgentSkillRepository.addCustom. - Closure path: external user work exposes a reusable workflow or skill defect → after the successful run the advisor explicitly reasons about future reuse/current skill coverage → it emits a create/update adaptation option → human accepts through the standard Chat UI → the first-party repository persists the new/updated enabled custom skill → future agent operation can use the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: HarnessLoop is wired with SkillEvolutionAdvisor in the packaged runtime and the standard Chat UI exposes apply/dismiss actions whose apply path persists the resulting skill; no repository-development workflow is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the advisor autonomously formulates an adaptation proposal, but the decisive right to alter persistent capability belongs to the human. Because that parent decision is first-party and returns into active skill capability, the published state is P, not A. - Evidence: SkillEvolutionAdvisor.kt, SkillSuggestionActions.kt, ChatViewModel.kt, current skill repository wiring. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic memory/learning and current-task web research are not the S4 witness. The credited witness is specifically the post-run future-reuse/skill-gap analysis plus human-governed persistent capability update. - External distinction: completed user-facing work reveals an externally sourced workflow demand or skill defect that the current reusable skill inventory does not adequately cover. - Future / prospective distinction: the advisor explicitly asks whether the just-observed workflow should be reusable in future tasks or whether an existing skill should be evolved for future operation. - Adaptation option generated: a normalized create or update skill proposal containing persistent future instructions, description and optional trigger. - Path back into current capability / S3: the human applies the proposal through the standard Chat UI; AgentSkillRepository.addCustom persists the new/updated enabled skill so subsequent agent operation can select/use the changed capability.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure is established at the assessed deployment recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate surfaces inspected included tool approvals, safety/permission modes, system prompts, model/provider settings, workflow approvals and human UI controls. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no path was found for an identity- or ultimate-policy-level issue to reach a legitimate authority and return as a durable highest-level policy/identity decision governing later S1–S4 operation. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ordinary human approvals, permission modes, static system prompts/configuration, session/model settings and workflow gates. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: ordinary approval of tools, rewinds, skill adaptation or workflows regulates action/capability but does not establish ultimate identity/policy closure. - Evidence: HarnessLoop.kt, approval/runtime policy surfaces, standard prompt/settings paths. - Basis: scoped absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cdf0b13df481624de8574fa9220e7e95bfc705d5/assessments/taixu.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cdf0b13df481624de8574fa9220e7e95bfc705d5\">cdf0b13 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-30T16:49:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gczhuang-cmd/NovaCode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NovaCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-30\">2026-10-30</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/gczhuang-cmd/NovaCode/tree/34a2d555a530120fd6241f7065d49390e52f3573\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 34a2d555a530120fd6241f7065d49390e52f3573\">34a2d55 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomous repository investigation and modification. - Disturbance / variety regulated: code defects, command results and changing local file contents. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next tool action or final response after observing prior results. - Decision owner: the main or worker model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: provider adapters, tool registry, permission engine, shell and conversation storage. - Closure path: model tool calls execute and results are appended to the conversation for the next request. - Boundary reachability: installed CLI registers the tools and constructs the first-party Agent; Team launches use that same implementation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model discretion leaves execution/permission machinery without the coding decisions. - Evidence: loop and result feedback, worker construction. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: source-backed availability, not successful provider-backed execution or coding performance.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: keep independent coding cells from overwriting one another&#39;s working files. - Disturbance / variety regulated: concurrent agents editing a shared checkout and reading stale parent paths. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select isolated worker execution and a later integration/conflict-resolution policy. - Decision owner: shipped worktree construction policy establishes separate execution contexts; a developer/operator must compose the discretionary coordination/integration actor beyond the enforced isolation path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Git worktrees, per-agent cwd injection, branch naming, change-preserving cleanup and mailbox/file locks. - Closure path: creation gives the worker a separate checkout and explicit local reread instruction; its tools run in that cwd and retained changes/branch return to the parent. - Boundary reachability: AgentTool&#39;s worktree branch and Team spawn explicitly call the supplied Worktree Manager; this is not a generic mailbox inference. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: worker isolation follows a preselected implementation/definition; no independent agent is shown owning the full interference-specific coordination policy merely because a Lead delegates. - Distinct S1 units: concurrently runnable Team members or parent and background operational agents. - Inter-S1 disturbance: shared-file write collision and stale reads between their repository operations. - Attenuating coordination relation: separate per-worker Git worktrees with cwd-context injection and preserved-result reporting. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: each worker is told to reread locally and operates in the isolated directory; the parent receives the retained worktree identity. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the implementation changes the actual editing substrate to prevent cross-worker overwrite; task dependencies and messaging alone are not credited. - Evidence: worktree execution and notices, Team worktree creation, stated concurrency purpose and integration boundary. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: worktrees do not resolve semantic merge conflicts; specification claims of autonomous convergence are not promoted into an independently established S2=A loop.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate current team commitments and permit or withhold worker implementation on behalf of the session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfinished/blocked team work, member readiness and proposed plans that need revision before changing code. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Lead reads team state, changes assignment/status/dependencies and approves or rejects a member plan with feedback. - Decision owner: the model-driven Team Lead. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: persistent task store, mailbox delivery, wakeup, lead-only approval check and worker permission/tool filtering. - Closure path: task/member notifications reach a Lead turn; its structured approval returns through the mailbox and changes worker permission mode/tools, while rejection requests a revised plan. - Boundary reachability: CLI registers TeamTask/TeamSendMessage and Team Agent spawn; TUI starts lead-mail consumption and wakeup. Optional Coordinator is supplied with these same tools. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Python enforces who may approve and applies the result; it does not choose whether a plan should be approved or what current commitment should change. - Whole-system current view: current Team tasks/status/assignees/dependencies, membership and member messages/idle notifications. - Current-control decision scope: current team work allocation and implementation permission, not identity or prospective strategic policy. - Evidence: CLI registration, task revision, Lead authority and delivery, returned plan effect, Lead feedback. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: cooperative agent judgment with bounded telemetry, not proof of global optimization; generic user approval and /team kill do not independently establish a parent S3 mode.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: optional source-grounded challenge to the coding session&#39;s changes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: errors/security defects omitted from the producer&#39;s account. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select and inspect raw changed files and judge defects in an independent-context review. - Decision owner: the forked review model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in skill catalog, fork executor, fresh Conversation, tool whitelist and report insertion. - Closure path: the review agent&#39;s final findings are inserted into the main conversation, available to subsequent coding turns and operator control. - Boundary reachability: the packaged built-in review skill is registered through the CLI skills path and executed by the shipped fork executor; no custom external reviewer must be built. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic fork/return machinery transports the independently reasoned findings rather than choosing defects or severity. - Claim being audited: the correctness and safety of repository changes made by the coding session. - Ordinary reporting path: the producer&#39;s main conversation/final narrative and tool reports. - Complementary access path: fresh reviewer uses git diff/status and direct file/reference reads. - Independence boundary: forkcontext none, separate conversation, explicit no-modification instruction; same provider/host is not a blind external audit. - Who acts on findings: parent coding session/operator; report enters subsequent control without automatic acceptance or guaranteed remediation. - Evidence: packaged review definition, fresh fork and return. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: optional bounded code audit; bash whitelist is not a security sandbox, and no independent human audit is claimed.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied external/prospective adaptation loop into present harness capability established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: memory/context and current task information, rather than future environmental change at this recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none evidenced for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: memory storage, compaction, skills/MCP loading and task planning. - Closure path: local context reuse is present; external distinction to adaptation option to current capability closure is absent. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: research/plan roles and persistence are not sufficient evidence of that organizational function. - Evidence: runtime context state, Coordinator current-task phases, architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: an operator can extend skills or choose new providers; that is not a shipped S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary action permissions and task plans. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity-level right routed and returned in the supplied mode. - Decision owner: operator/developer configures rules; Lead approves operational plans. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: blacklist, sandbox, permission modes, hooks and Coordinator feature gates. - Closure path: action/plan permissions affect tools, but are not identity/ultimate-policy deliberation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: neither static safety rules nor Lead operational approval establishes S5. - Evidence: mode configuration, plan return, permission architecture. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: users legitimately own their tasks; no general claim about their wider organization follows.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/6102e73ec2bf24b8672a7bedd87657225de7fe37/assessments/no/novacode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 6102e73ec2bf24b8672a7bedd87657225de7fe37\">6102e73 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T10:15:21+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/caelis-labs/caelis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Caelis</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/caelis-labs/caelis/tree/5aebbc6a750b8b2d472a0987fbf2e0942062c6d9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5aebbc6a750b8b2d472a0987fbf2e0942062c6d9\">5aebbc6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed engineering work through repeated model-selected tool actions against the workspace and other admitted resources, incorporating tool/environment observations until the task concludes. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, repository/workspace state, command and file outcomes, external tool/provider responses, approval/sandbox conditions, task failures and follow-up input. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next substantive model/tool action and its task-specific arguments, then revise later action from returned evidence. - Decision owner: the active Caelis Agent model in the main Runtime; each started collaborator independently owns the same local operational discretion within its assigned task. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent SDK Runtime, tool definitions/execution wrappers, Session/event persistence, sandbox/policy and approval gates, task lifecycle, compaction and Control-owned runtime composition. - Closure path: user/session context → model chooses action/tool → Runtime validates and executes or requests approval → result/event is persisted and returned into model context → the Agent chooses its next action or final response. - Boundary reachability: this is the normal shipped Caelis Runtime assembled by the product; no eval or developer-only runner is required to exercise the model/tool loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy/sandbox/approval machinery can constrain an attempted action, but removing the model removes the task-specific choice of what operational action to take next and how to respond to its result. - Evidence: agent-sdk/runtime/runtime.go; app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate interference among concurrently operating participant S1 units by limiting parallel delegation to independent work, assigning task-specific scope/edit authority and allowing explicit steering/integration when participant work interacts. - Disturbance / variety regulated: multiple participant Agents can otherwise perform dependent or overlapping work in the same delivery workspace, duplicate effort, act on stale assumptions or create conflicting effects/results that cannot be safely integrated. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide which work is sufficiently independent to run as a collaborator, define its self-contained goal/scope/constraints/edit permission, and send task-specific follow-up/steering when participant findings require coordination. - Decision owner: the main controller Agent model. The shipped prompt makes independence and integration its responsibility; the StartThread schema exposes the scope/edit-permission decision to that model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Control-authenticated participant topology, Session execution fencing, revision/fence requirements for overlapping writes, StartThread, SendMessage, shared mailbox/roster, public-result observation and durable Session/task identities. - Closure path: controller model identifies independent work and specifies scope/edit permission → participant S1 executes within that assignment → shared messages or thread outcomes expose relevant interaction/results → controller can steer with SendMessage, wait/read the participant and change subsequent participant/main work → controller performs integration/validation before user delivery. - Boundary reachability: the independence rule is injected into the shipped main-Agent system prompt and StartThread/mail/thread tools are part of the first-party collaboration runtime; no downstream coordinator is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic fencing/revision rules enforce effect safety, but they do not decide task-specific independence, participant scope or how returned findings should alter concurrent work. Those coordination judgments belong to the controller model. - Evidence: app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; agent-sdk/tool/builtin/spawn/tool.go; control/collaboration/tools.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: Caelis does not expose TaiXu-style per-child write leases or a global conflict scheduler. S2 credit rests on the shipped model-owned independent-work/scope/steering relation plus concrete shared-workspace effect fencing, not on generic messaging alone. - Distinct S1 units: the main controller Agent plus two or more independently running participant Agent conversations created with StartThread, each executing its own assigned task and returning a public result. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent or overlapping participant work can duplicate effort, use conflicting assumptions or attempt overlapping workspace effects; the runtime explicitly treats overlapping writes as requiring a matching revision/fence. - Attenuating coordination relation: the controller is instructed to spawn only independent work, defines each participant&#39;s scope/edit permission, can exchange targeted messages/steering and retains integration/validation responsibility; runtime revision/fence rules enforce concurrent effect consistency. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: participant mail/status/results return through SendMessage/ReadThread/WaitThread; the controller uses them to steer or revise later participant/main action and to decide what can be integrated into final delivery. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the credited path is not the mailbox by itself; it is the explicit independent-work and scope/edit-permission relation tied to a structurally evidenced shared-workspace concurrency disturbance and returned steering/integration feedback.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: maintain current control over the Session&#39;s active participant organization by creating commitments, observing current participant state/results, steering work and deciding how participant outcomes are integrated into the live user task. - Disturbance / variety regulated: changing participant roster/state, running versus blocked/attention-needing/completed work, partial or conflicting findings, participant capabilities, newly discovered facts and the need to reallocate current task commitments. - Decisive decision or feedback right: create or withhold participant commitments, select participant/task/scope, observe live thread state/results, send follow-up/steering, wait for selected work and decide whether to integrate, redirect or continue the current job. - Decision owner: the main controller Agent model for task-specific current-control decisions; Control authenticates the current controller epoch and enforces exclusive controller capabilities. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ListThreads, controller-only ReadThread/WaitThread, StartThread, SendMessage, current-controller grants, participant/task state, Session persistence, Control-owned placement/lifecycle and controller-epoch enforcement. - Closure path: controller creates a set of current participant commitments → participants execute and update status/results/messages → controller observes roster plus selected current results/attention states → controller steers, waits, launches changed work or integrates outcomes → later S1 execution follows the revised current-control decision. - Boundary reachability: controller-only capabilities are wired into the standard first-party collaboration surface and injected into the parent Agent; they are not test-only lifecycle APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Control owns topology security, placement and controller selection, but does not choose the task-specific portfolio, delegation content, steering or integration response. Removing the controller model leaves enforcement but not materially the same discretionary current-control decisions. - Evidence: control/collaboration/controller.go; control/collaboration/tools.go; docs/participants.md; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: model-facing collaboration does not expose an arbitrary hard kill/reassignment API; S3 is established through commitment creation, current whole-roster/thread observation, steering and integration authority rather than deterministic cancellation machinery. - Whole-system current view: ListThreads exposes the Session participant roster; controller-only ReadThread returns a participant&#39;s latest public result and status, while WaitThread waits across selected threads for completion or attention. Together these let the controller inspect the current operational portfolio rather than only a final batch result. - Current-control decision scope: choose active participant commitments and their task/scope, send steering or follow-up input where supported, wait/read selected work, start changed/additional participant work and decide how current outputs affect the main task&#39;s integration and completion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: independently review current workspace changes through a separate Reviewer Agent/context rather than relying only on the main Agent&#39;s own completion/reporting path. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the main operational Agent may miss correctness defects, regressions, maintainability/architecture issues or inadequate tests in the current staged/unstaged/untracked workspace delta. - Decisive decision or feedback right: inspect the current workspace change from the fixed Reviewer scene and produce a separate findings judgment that can challenge the main Agent&#39;s ordinary delivery assessment. - Decision owner: the configured Reviewer model or ACP Agent owns the review judgment once invoked; however, standard audit initiation is the user /review path, so an autonomous system-owned invocation/closure is not established at this ref. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: fixed reviewer scene/instructions, separately materialized Reviewer placement, persistent background Reviewer Task, separate child context and follow-up routing. - Closure path: user invokes /review → Control starts a persistent Reviewer with the fixed workspace-review prompt → Reviewer independently inspects and reports findings → the result is available in the same Session for human/controller follow-up and can change subsequent work. The first-party audit path exists, but autonomous invocation is left open. - Boundary reachability: /review is a shipped Caelis control surface and Reviewer materialization is first-party runtime code supporting either a configured model or ACP Agent; no repository CI reviewer is borrowed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the audit judgment itself is made by a distinct Reviewer Agent rather than deterministic parsing, but the ordinary path does not give the operating organization an independent autonomous right to trigger that audit. This is therefore C, not A. - Evidence: internal/controlprompt/review.go; internal/controlprompt/appserveradapter/participant.go; app/gatewayapp/reviewscene.go; docs/participants.md. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: Guardian is not credited here; the architecture explicitly limits Guardian to approval/risk review and states that it does not independently audit task completion. - Claim being audited: that the current workspace delta is correct, regression-safe, maintainable, architecturally sound and sufficiently tested for delivery. - Ordinary reporting path: the main Agent executes the user task, performs its own verification/integration and returns the user-facing result through its normal model/tool Session. - Complementary access path: a separately materialized Reviewer Agent receives a fixed prompt to inspect staged, unstaged and untracked workspace changes and lead with concrete findings. - Independence boundary: separate Reviewer model/ACP placement, background Task and retained child context; it is not the main Agent merely re-reading its own final answer. Invocation remains user-owned at the standard /review surface. - Who acts on findings: the human/controller can continue the Reviewer and change subsequent main/participant work based on findings; the standard path does not itself grant Reviewer authority to commit or close the parent task.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party external-and-prospective organizational adaptation loop was established at the reviewed standard-distribution boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established for an S4 loop. Caelis can search/use external tools and can organize memory, but those mechanisms serve current work or internal recall rather than a future-oriented environmental adaptation function. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for changing present organizational capability from an external/prospective adaptation judgment. - Decision owner: none established for S4 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP/tool discovery, configurable models/agents, skills, memory Remember/Recall, optional Memory Steward and Memory Verifier, runtime configuration snapshots and ordinary human configuration. - Closure path: no qualifying external/prospective signal → adaptation option → present capability change closure was found in the shipped runtime. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: tool selection, retrieval, semantic memory organization and provider configuration can improve current execution context, but no evidenced first-party actor owns a distinct outside-and-then adaptation judgment whose result changes the organization&#39;s future capability. - Evidence: docs/architecture.md; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md; app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure was established at the reviewed Session recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: static identity, user objectives, sandbox/approval constraints, controller selection and configuration are present, but no runtime issue path decides what the organization ultimately is or which ultimate policy should govern it. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S5 identity/ultimate-policy judgment. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: built-in identity/workflow prompts, user/workspace instruction precedence, sandbox/policy registry, Guardian/manual approvals, Control-owned controller selection/handoff and operator configuration. - Closure path: ordinary user/configuration/approval inputs can constrain subsequent actions, but no qualifying identity/ultimate-policy issue is routed to a legitimate S5 authority and returned as authoritative organizational policy closure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the main model interprets tasks within a predefined identity/policy envelope; it does not own authority to redefine that envelope. Control enforces controller/policy state but no first-party runtime process establishes a separate ultimate-policy judgment loop. - Evidence: app/gatewayapp/internal/promptassembly/prompt.go; docs/agent-sdk-boundary.md; docs/architecture.md. - Basis: explicit + structural absence -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5904d1035156475161d7e84a724fb41efdf138f9/assessments/caelis.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5904d1035156475161d7e84a724fb41efdf138f9\">5904d10 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T01:25:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DeepSeek Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness/tree/ddefc45fbc7f8e46dd73185e68295696d1297887\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ddefc45fbc7f8e46dd73185e68295696d1297887\">ddefc45 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A. - Function: autonomously pursue repository/general tasks through repeated model decisions, tool calls, Session state and subsequent turns. - Decisive right / owner: the model-driven Agent chooses operational actions from the supplied tool surface. - Closure: accepted tool/results and Session events enter later model requests and alter subsequent action. - Supporting mechanisms: Agent loop, Session persistence, tool registry, provider adapters, cancellation and optional subagents. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A. - Positive mode: published opt-in Agent Teams. - Distinct S1 units: Team Lead plus named teammate Agents working in one shared checkout. - Concrete disturbance: concurrent members can contend over shared files and stale task/assignment state. The first-party design explicitly notes that Bash, generators and external writers can bypass file stale-version fences, so coordination rather than false locking is required. - Coordination relation: - shared tasks have owners, dependency DAGs, readiness and advisory write scopes; - blocked tasks cannot be claimed until dependencies complete; - stale task revisions fail instead of silently overwriting newer assignment state; - overlapping in-progress write scopes are surfaced as warnings; - peer messages steer running members or resume idle/inactive teammates; - the fixed Team policy directs members to partition writes, order dependent work and rebase after stale-file feedback. - Feedback closure: board/list/get results, overlap warnings, rejected stale mutations and peer messages return into Team-member model contexts; members subsequently claim/release/complete work, message peers, or alter task structure. - Decisive right / owner: Agents choose task decomposition, dependencies, write scopes, claims and peer coordination; deterministic CAS/DAG/mailbox machinery enforces those decisions. The organizational coordination right is therefore agent-owned, not merely runtime-owned. - Caveat: write scopes are advisory rather than filesystem locks, so S2 attenuates shared-checkout interference without eliminating every external-write collision. -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A(P). - Autonomous Team mode: - the Lead can inspect roster/task state; - only the Lead creates and interrupts teammates; - the Lead may assign/reassign tasks across members; - the Team policy requires the Lead to wait for required work, inspect the final diff and run tests before the final answer. - Whole-system current disturbance: active/inactive/stuck workers, assignment gaps, dependency readiness, overlapping intended writes and incomplete Team commitments. - Agent-owned closure: the Lead observes current Team state and can spawn, message, assign/reassign, interrupt, wait and integrate; those interventions change subsequent teammate work and Team commitments. This establishes A. - Parent mode: the published Agent Teams Web profile lets a user inspect the authoritative roster/task board and create, edit, assign/unassign, complete, reopen and delete Team tasks. Mutations use revision-checked Remote methods and the UI reloads the complete Team view after success or conflict. Agents subsequently read the same authoritative task board, so parent decisions return into current operation. This separately establishes first-party parent-governed S3 and yields A(P). - Supporting/enforcement mechanisms: Team service, task CAS/DAG validation, Remote API, browser Client and Session persistence. - Caveat: the Web parent panel cannot itself spawn or interrupt teammates; its parent S3 authority is task/commitment regulation rather than the full Lead lifecycle surface. -\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: —. - A teammate can be asked to review a diff, but generic role delegation does not by itself supply a distinct independent audit function. - Experimental Auto Review performs a separate model call before each pending tool body and gates execution according to risk/authorization policy. This is pre-action permission control, not complementary independent access to operational reality after or outside the normal control channel. - Ralph explicitly states that completion/blocker outcomes are worker reports and are not independently verified; its documentation defers an independent evaluator. - No first-party mode was established that supplies a sufficiently independent audit actor with its own operational evidence and audit-to-correction closure. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: —. - Web search, goals, workflows, Ralph rounds, Session history and plugin composition can change a current task trajectory. None establishes a distinct first-party function that monitors the external future environment, develops organizational adaptation options and closes them into changed future capability. - Plugin installation/update is operator/deployment configuration rather than autonomous S4. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: —. - Permission presets, Auto Review risk rules, system/persona prompts, plugin/profile selection and explicit-human Team activation are constraints and deployment choices. They do not establish a runtime identity/ultimate-policy function that resolves organizational policy tensions at the declared recursion. - Generic human approval or profile choice is not promoted to parent S5. -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/b293e7031f149c5f41baedede15b444f983a5f4d/assessments/deepseek-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit b293e7031f149c5f41baedede15b444f983a5f4d\">b293e70 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-20T21:45:19+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/i3T4AN/KADATH\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">KADATH</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-20\">2026-09-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/i3T4AN/KADATH/tree/db7a6438d98c18d590b78b2146dc3bcd2c4ea0ef\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision db7a6438d98c18d590b78b2146dc3bcd2c4ea0ef\">db7a643 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: heritable organism agents perform the user goal by reasoning, using tools, managing durable progress, producing artifacts/results, and optionally delegating bounded subproblems to temporary workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended task/environment variety encountered while trying to satisfy the approved measurement criterion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next reasoning/tool/workspace action and determine the candidate operational result within the organism&#39;s allowed capabilities. - Decision owner: the organism&#39;s model-driven agent loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: read-only genome mount, scoped broker token, workspace/artifact storage, browser/search tools, worker limits, epoch deadline, resource/container isolation, and the kernel&#39;s lifecycle enforcement. - Closure path: observations and tool/worker results return to the organism; the organism updates durable progress/output and continues acting until the epoch finishes. - Boundary reachability: organism execution is the standard population runtime created for every approved KADATH run; it does not rely on a development-only or external harness owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the kernel constrains the action space but does not choose the organism&#39;s task-solving actions; those choices are model-driven inside the shipped organism loop. - Evidence: inside an epoch and worker behavior, research, browsing, and temporary workers. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: temporary workers are subordinate to a parent organism and are not separately credited as S1 units at the declared recursion.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party inter-S1 coordination function is established at the declared population recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not applicable. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: per-organism containers, scoped credentials, separate browser contexts, population ranking, and sequencing isolate or compare organisms but do not by themselves regulate a demonstrated interference/oscillation among cooperating S1 units. - Closure path: not established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: population plurality, competition, shared benchmark use, and isolation are insufficient to establish S2 without a specific inter-S1 disturbance and attenuation loop. - Evidence: population execution and isolation, isolation and credentials. - Basis: structural negative finding. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deployment could build cooperative coordination among organisms, but the reviewed standard distribution does not establish the required S2-specific witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: the kernel maintains a whole-run current-control path over population status and applies ranking, survival/replacement, execution limits, and activation decisions that regulate the current evolutionary organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: population failure, uneven verified performance, invalid/duplicate adaptation, runaway execution, and loss of a viable active population. - Decisive decision or feedback right: determine which verified genomes remain active, which are culled/replaced, and which valid post-grade adaptations become the next active population under the fixed current-control policy. - Decision owner: the standard runtime exposes this S3-specific control path through developer-authored deterministic kernel policy; no autonomous agent owns the decisive population-control policy in the reviewed mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen grading results, deterministic score/rank computation, population-size rules, content-addressed genomes, worktree validation, resource limits, and activation/cleanup machinery. - Closure path: verified outcomes and ranks enter the kernel selection path; the selected survivors/adaptations/children become the active population for the next epoch. - Boundary reachability: the selection/activation path is built into the standard kernel and runs after every non-final graded epoch; it is not a CI or example-only construction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tweaker/Birther and middle agents can propose adaptations, but the whole-population current-control right remains in fixed kernel selection/activation rules. KADATH therefore supplies a concrete S3 construction path without an autonomous S3 owner. - Whole-system current view: the kernel has run/epoch identities, frozen grading outcomes, ranks, lineage, population membership, crashes, and active genome state for the whole population. - Current-control decision scope: population survival/replacement/activation and enforcement of run-wide execution constraints; deterministic enforcement is recorded separately from model-driven adaptation proposals. - Evidence: evidence freezing and grading, selection and evolution, kernel implementation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: C does not credit hard enforcement as autonomous management. It records the first-party function-specific current-control path while preserving that the decisive policy is developer-authored rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separate model Grader inspects a frozen evidence boundary rather than ordinary organism self-report, makes rubric/anti-fraud/failure judgments, and returns those findings into population control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: fabricated, unsupported, fraudulent, crashed, or otherwise invalid claims of task success and misleading organism self-scores. - Decisive decision or feedback right: make the semantic audit judgments for rubric facts, automatic failures, anti-fraud checks, tie-break facts, and cited evidence from the frozen attempt. - Decision owner: the model-driven Grader. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel-owned evidence freezing, fragment coverage/checksums, reference validation, integrity seals, and deterministic locked formula application. - Closure path: Grader findings are validated by the kernel, converted through locked formulas into verified outcome/score/rank, and directly affect selection, culling, and reproduction for the next population. - Boundary reachability: the Grader/frozen-evidence path is part of every standard grading phase, separate from the evolvable organism genome and reachable without borrowing a development-only reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the kernel does not itself make the semantic evidence judgments; it freezes evidence, validates references, and applies formulas. Removing the Grader leaves those discretionary audit judgments absent. - Claim being audited: the organism&#39;s claimed task outcome and evidence satisfy the approved benchmark without triggering failure/anti-fraud rules. - Ordinary reporting path: organism result envelope, workspace/artifacts, high-level activity, tool/model traces, worker outputs, and provisional result material produced during execution. - Complementary access path: kernel-created immutable frozen attempt including artifacts, traces, browser/worker evidence, crash/identity metadata, exact objective/genome hashes, and optional independent measurement facts. - Independence boundary: the Grader operates after all parents/workers are stopped, against frozen evidence; organisms cannot rewrite the benchmark, score, scheduler, lineage, or frozen post-run evidence and self-scores are explicitly ignored. - Who acts on findings: the kernel validates the audit references, computes locked scores/ranks, and uses them in population selection and reproduction. - Evidence: evidence freezing and grading, selection and evolution. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the deterministic kernel owns formula application and enforcement, not the model Grader&#39;s audit judgment; this split is why the audit is not inferred merely from the component name Grader.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: the evolutionary adaptation loop turns verified environment/benchmark performance and evidence from the current population into changed agent frameworks for future epochs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: mismatch between present organism capability and the externally measured performance demanded by the locked task environment/benchmark. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose adaptation proposals for future capability: middle organisms may mutate or remain unchanged; Tweaker/Birther paths derive reproduction guidance and mutated children from verified population evidence. - Decision owner: model-driven organism/specialist adaptation actors within the shipped evolutionary loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: kernel grading/ranking, elite dossiers and snapshots, population/own/inherited memory, mutation validation, duplicate rejection, Git lineage, and next-epoch activation. - Closure path: verified performance/evidence informs model-driven mutation/reproduction choices; valid changed genomes are committed and activated; subsequent epochs execute those changed capabilities against the environment. - Boundary reachability: middle-agent reflection and Tweaker/Birther reproduction are standard post-grade phases of the shipped run rather than separate development tooling. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic selection and mutation safeguards constrain candidates, but the substantive adaptation proposals are produced by model-driven actors from verified evidence. - External distinction: observed, frozen, independently graded performance and evidence under the approved benchmark/tool/environment configuration. - Future / prospective distinction: which framework characteristics, memories, and mutations are likely to improve performance in subsequent epochs. - Adaptation option generated: preserve, mutate prompt/source/dependencies/files, or reproduce a mutated descendant from an elite genome. - Path back into current capability / S3: the kernel validates and commits accepted mutations, restores the configured population, and activates the changed genome set for the next epoch. - Evidence: middle-agent reflection, Tweaker and Birther, memory and heredity. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this credit is not based on the label evolution; it depends on a closed future-capability loop from measured environment performance to changed genomes that actually run later.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: establish and lock the run-level definition of success, permitted evidence/tools, anti-fraud constraints, and measurement policy that governs every organism and evolutionary selection decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ambiguity or drift in what the evolutionary organization is optimizing and what evidence/behavior counts as legitimate success. - Decisive decision or feedback right: approve or reject the Architect&#39;s proposed benchmark before activation; once approved, the benchmark/objective/tool/runtime hashes are locked for the run. - Decision owner: the human operator as parent authority for the run. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Architect proposal generation, structured benchmark contract, preflight, immutable hash locks, and runtime refusal to continue after locked-input tampering. - Closure path: operator approval activates and locks the policy; the locked benchmark subsequently governs grading, ranking, selection, and all future epochs. Rejection leaves the proposed run inactive. - Boundary reachability: benchmark proposal and explicit approval are part of the standard interactive launch path, not repository governance or an adjacent development process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the Architect proposes policy content but does not hold ultimate activation authority; there is no evidenced standard mode in which an agent can unilaterally redefine and authorize the run&#39;s ultimate fitness policy. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: what the whole evolutionary run exists to optimize, what measurements define success, and which anti-fraud/tool/evidence constraints bind the population. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: parent mode only — the human operator approves the benchmark that becomes authoritative for the run. - Return-to-operation path: approved benchmark and hashes become immutable runtime inputs to grading, ranking, and evolutionary selection across all epochs. - Evidence: Architect and benchmark locking, run setup and approval. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: ordinary organism result approval is not being called S5. The credited parent decision is the run-wide, persistent fitness/legitimacy policy that controls the identity of the evolutionary process.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df/assessments/kadath.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c9da28372ac35ff5d8bc9a74679a6987a5d451df\">c9da283 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T19:38:34+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickprotop/cxagent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CXAgent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/nickprotop/cxagent/tree/e2b2551fc8897d5be5833441e9ec6e3c4e63cdf1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e2b2551fc8897d5be5833441e9ec6e3c4e63cdf1\">e2b2551 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: interpret a coding request, read/modify files, run tools/tests, handle failures and adapt the next step. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-specific requirements, repository contents, tests and shell/tool results, permissions, model messages, tool errors and background task outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select next tool action or delegate child operation and revise based on observed result. - Decision owner: root model in a normal coding session; bounded child models in fan-out mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: .NET host, model provider transport, session persistence, file/shell job executor, permission layer and child spawner. - Closure path: operator request → root model → first-party permission-gated tool/delegate action → file/test/child feedback → model correction or output. - Boundary reachability: included in the supported executable cxagent terminal session, and in CxAgent.Core as composed by that session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a model chooses task-specific actions and responses; deterministic host only executes and constrains them. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentHost.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileJobExecutor.cs, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: third-party model/provider is the decision engine reached through first-party harness, not a separate imported organization.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: attenuate concurrent S1 code-writing interference on the same workspace file, making incompatible stale edits visible instead of silently losing one worker&#39;s change. - Distinct S1 units: independent concurrently executing model-driven child agents instantiated by a root session and sharing the shipped file executor/workspace. - Inter-S1 disturbance: simultaneous children can read one file, form edits on the same original bytes, then overwrite one another; the first-party commentary records this and a real concurrent-replace regression test. - Attenuating coordination relation: a static per-canonical-path lock spans the entire read-modify-write and stale-content validation; WriteIfUnchangedAsync refuses obsolete edits. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: a stale edit produces a specific refusal explaining that nothing was written and instructing reread/revise; that tool result enters the child&#39;s model feedback loop, allowing a new edit against current bytes, and parent sees outcomes. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the mechanism directly handles conflicting writes by distinct simultaneous coding S1s on one shared artifact, not merely dispatching messages or serializing unrelated jobs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: races in shared-file mutation and loss of independent children&#39;s implemented changes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: coordinate which writer may hold the file path, reject stale operation and return corrective signal; agent chooses subsequent remediation, not lock policy itself. - Decision owner: first-party deterministic FileMutation chooses lock/admission; autonomous children can respond to returned conflict, but the S2-specific policy choice is not dynamically agent owned. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: static SemaphoreSlim map, file snapshots, StaleContentException, shared FileJobExecutor. - Closure path: competing child edits → per-path exclusion/validation → conflict refusal or sequential safe result → next child-model correction on returned evidence. - Boundary reachability: first-party normal replaceinfile/write operations and fan-out children share this executable file mutation path; not a dev-only test helper. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the structural interference response is supplied and selected by deterministic shipped code; C credits a function-specific constructor path, not autonomous S2 policy ownership. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileMutation.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/Builtin/FileJobExecutor.cs, cxagent.Tests/TwoSessionsTests.cs, cxagent.Tests/FileJobExecutorTests.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: per-file safety is narrower than negotiated scheduling over whole-repository edits. Across distinct processes the in-process lock does not apply; stale-content check remains a separate guard.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: within one multi-child session, inspect currently committed child/background work and intervene on present operational commitments or available resources. - Whole-system current view: model-facing agentlist reports retained child identities, specialist types and busy state; joblist reports all live background commands, ownership, timing, output paths and concurrency use across the session. - Current-control decision scope: root model may use agentsend to redirect an existing child with preserved context, deliver to busy child mailbox, or kill any session/child-created background job rather than simply wait for completion; it can choose follow-on delegation and priority in response to statuses. - Disturbance / variety regulated: runaway/surplus background work, stale or blocked commitments, changing task priorities and subsidiary agents requiring correction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select operational intervention, new instruction or job termination using whole-session current facts, not only run a static queue. - Decision owner: autonomous root model when it invokes the bundled live view/control tools in fan-out mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child store, busy-claim mutex, mailbox, process registry, runtime permission and job ownership checks. - Closure path: child/work registry state → agentlist/joblist → root model&#39;s follow-up/jobkill decision → child mailbox/future turn or terminated background process → updated current operation. - Boundary reachability: all named tools are offered by first-party root-agent runtime in the shipped fan-out/session mode, not hypothetical external dashboard APIs. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: parent chooses which child to redirect or process to stop, while runtime only keeps the list/ownership boundaries and enforces specific actions. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentReachTools.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentStore.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/AgentMailbox.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Jobs/BackgroundJobTools.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium. - Caveats: synchronous tool result barrier means the model may not inspect every executing child continuously during the original spawn turn; the credited current-control path is session-local, based on retained children, mailbox and concurrent background jobs. Not global control across separate UI sessions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: a separately running bundled review child checks actual source/diff evidence and challenges the implementer&#39;s correctness claim before the parent acts on findings. - Claim being audited: another agent or root implementer has produced code that is correct for the requested change. - Ordinary reporting path: author&#39;s own completion report, observed tool/test outputs and normal coding trace. - Complementary access path: distinct first-party child Agent with fresh conversation, builtin review type and direct read/grep/shell tools inspects named changed files or diff and returns specific objections with failing cases. - Independence boundary: the built-in type is supplied by product code, receives its own model context and can inspect artifact bytes rather than relying solely on original developer&#39;s narration. It may use the same base provider/model; independence here is execution/context and evidence access, not guaranteed distinct vendor. - Who acts on findings: root model receives review subagent result in normal tool feedback and may modify code, run checks or redirect work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: silent correctness defects, incomplete implementation and overconfident author claims. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose an artifact-grounded judgment independently of implementation context, return objections as new information for corrective control. - Decision owner: reviewer child model for its audit judgment; parent owns corrective task choices. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: builtin review profile/type, separate child context, shared read tools, first-party subagent-result envelope and parent continuation. - Closure path: parent requests independent review → first-party reviewer reads operational artifacts → model reports concrete verified issues → parent model receives and can correct subsequent implementation. - Boundary reachability: review is a shipped built-in agent type in BuiltinAgentTypes.cs; ordinary agent subagent factory/spawner can instantiate it in product&#39;s standard fan-out mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child model makes the differential correctness judgment from direct workspace evidence; deterministic return path does not choose its findings. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/BuiltinAgentTypes.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentSpawner.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/SubAgentFactory.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: role invocation is optional and parent-chosen, not an always-on merge admission or externally guaranteed neutral auditor. Distinct context and raw file access, not naming alone, underpin this classification.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no evidenced first-party closed external-prospective adaptation loop changing the system&#39;s future organizational capability at this chosen session boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as organizational S4; task-local web fetch, model selection and extension loading address present work or developer/user-supplied capabilities. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no supported agent-owned future-environment interpretation and returned capability adaptation identified. - Decision owner: no S4 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web/HTTP fetch tools, session context, model catalogs, skills and plugin loading, user configuration, project docs and development roadmap. - Closure path: none qualifying S4; local task results inform immediate coding decisions rather than durable future capability design owned by the agent. - Boundary reachability: no positive S4 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: externally authored extensions and model/provider changes can alter capability but do not prove an agent-led prospective outside-and-then loop in the supported distribution. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Llm/ProviderCatalog.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/Agent.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Sessions/Session.cs, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: optional contributor-authored/adaptive plugins not included unless actually wired in a first-party shipped autonomous mode.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party organizational constitutional arbiter for the system&#39;s enduring purpose/ultimate policy, with authoritative feedback into operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission/trust exceptions and task constraints exist, but identity- or mission-level policy conflicts are not evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none meeting S5 ultimate purpose/identity at selected session recursion. - Decision owner: none established; user and config supply ordinary execution trust and tool permissions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: folder permissions, trusted commands, model classifier in auto mode, builtin agent type briefings, user-defined config and plugin gates. - Closure path: runtime enforces user-set local rules; no identity/purpose proposal → legitimate authority → constitutional decision → returned policy loop found. - Boundary reachability: no positive S5 claim. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing model still leaves configuration and guard rules; removing operator consent does not expose autonomous ultimate-purpose decision right. - Evidence: cxagent.Core/Core/Permissions/PermissionPolicy.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Permissions/PermissionDecider.cs, cxagent.Core/Core/Agents/BuiltinAgentTypes.cs, COMMANDS.md, README.md. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external project/human governance is outside this standard session boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2b9b27db39203953df10c81d89bef53e30246e99/assessments/cxagent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2b9b27db39203953df10c81d89bef53e30246e99\">2b9b27d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"3\" data-a=\"3\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-04T16:23:59+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thinkany-ai/dscode\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DSCode</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"3\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>3</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-04\">2026-10-04</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/thinkany-ai/dscode/tree/1ce0328cfa856700f6c955f5429ca00b08d99ea5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 1ce0328cfa856700f6c955f5429ca00b08d99ea5\">1ce0328 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform environment-facing software-engineering work through a model-driven coding session that selects tools, observes results and iterates. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, repository/file/Git state, command/test/diagnostic output, tool failures, model observations, context pressure, permissions and implementation choices. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose what evidence to inspect, what coding/command/patch/delegation action to perform next, how to revise work from results and when to finish the task. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed DSCode coding agent in the supported session runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: DSCode Core tool registration; minimal/safe toolsets; patch/checkpoint path; command sandbox; permissions; sessions; plan state; diagnostics; MCP/hooks; CLI/Desktop/RPC hosts. - Closure path: user objective + current session/workspace → model selects tool/action → DSCode executes/gates it → result returns into the coding session → model chooses subsequent action or completes. - Boundary reachability: normal terminal, headless/RPC and Desktop surfaces instantiate the same DSCode Core and coding session without requiring downstream orchestration. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-backed primary actor leaves tools, permission/sandbox and session machinery but removes the open-ended coding judgment that selects and sequences actions. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts; packages/core/src/tools.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: provider inference and generic coding-session substrate are dependencies; DSCode is credited for the concrete first-party product composition and organizational roles it exposes.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: attenuate destructive write interference among concurrently executing delegated implementation S1s by assigning them independent Git worktrees. - Disturbance / variety regulated: two or more implementation children editing the same checkout in parallel could overwrite, race with or observe one another&#39;s incomplete changes, making candidate ownership/integration unreliable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose which independent delegated tasks receive the implementer role, whose standard semantics include isolated candidate changes rather than shared-workspace mutation. - Decision owner: the primary model-backed DSCode agent calling the standard delegate tool. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: role-bearing delegate schema; explicit model prompt guidelines; four-worker bounded fan-out; per-implementer detached Git worktree creation; per-child cwd; returned candidate diffs; depth-one child boundary. - Closure path: primary agent identifies independent parallel work → assigns implementation tasks to implementer children → DSCode creates a separate worktree for each before execution → children modify isolated repository states → each returns its result/diff → primary agent uses the coordinated outputs for subsequent integration/verification. - Boundary reachability: registerSubagentTools registers delegate in the normal DSCode Core and the README documents it as standard parallel work; no external team orchestrator is required. - Distinct S1 units: two or more concurrently launched implementer child DSCode coding sessions, each capable of bounded model/tool software-engineering work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: parallel implementation children would otherwise share mutable repository state and can overwrite or contaminate one another&#39;s in-progress file changes. - Attenuating coordination relation: DSCode maps the model-selected implementer role to a separate detached Git worktree for each child, removing the shared-write surface while preserving an explicit candidate diff for integration. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: isolation changes each child S1&#39;s actual working environment before it acts; after completion the primary S1 receives every child result/diff and can choose which candidate to integrate or rework. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the positive mapping rests on a concrete cross-S1 write-collision disturbance and a first-party isolation relation that specifically attenuates that disturbance. Role names, concurrency and delegation alone are not credited. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: DSCode deterministically enforces implementer isolation, but the primary model owns the task-specific decision to classify/delegate independent work as implementation tasks under that coordination mode. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: explorer/reviewer/tester children do not need the same write isolation. S2=A credits the supported agent-owned role/decomposition decision that invokes the interference-specific implementer path.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-organization current-control loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the primary agent decomposes work and later receives child outcomes, but reviewed evidence does not expose live descendant-state observation plus substantive mid-run steering/reallocation/commitment control across the child organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established at S3 level. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: delegate task decomposition; concurrency cap; child success/failure/result fan-in; primary integration responsibility; plan/status; permissions and background processes. - Closure path: not applicable; no whole-system current view → current-control intervention → changed active child commitments/resources loop was reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: initial delegation and final integration remain part of the primary S1&#39;s execution/decomposition. The parent waits for bounded child results rather than acting as a live current-control center over them. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: “the primary agent owns integration and final validation” is meaningful responsibility but does not by itself establish the Profile&#39;s whole-current S3 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: A - Function: independently review a producing coding S1&#39;s change using a separate read-only model-backed reviewer and return findings to the primary agent. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the primary/implementer coding path may produce a change that contains correctness defects, regressions or unsupported assumptions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: independently inspect repository/change evidence in a read-only child context and return a reviewer judgment prioritizing correctness/regressions. - Decision owner: the separate model-backed DSCode child selected with role reviewer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model-callable delegate; reviewer role instructions; child DSCode process; --permission plan; --sandbox read-only; independent child context; result aggregation into the parent tool response. - Closure path: producing S1 has a candidate/change → primary delegates a reviewer task → separate read-only reviewer inspects current repository evidence → reviewer returns findings → DSCode delivers those findings in the delegate result → primary agent can revise/integrate/reject subsequent work. - Boundary reachability: reviewer is a built-in standard role in the normal delegate tool and is documented in the shipped product; no custom downstream reviewer code is required. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime launches and confines the reviewer, while the semantic review judgment comes from a distinct model-backed child; removing that child removes the complementary audit judgment. - Evidence: packages/core/src/subagents.ts; README.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: review invocation is model-selected rather than mandatory for every task. - Claim being audited: the correctness/fitness of a code change or candidate implementation produced by the primary/implementation path. - Ordinary reporting path: normal primary/implementer coding output and candidate diff/result. - Complementary access path: a separate reviewer process with read-only permission/sandbox independently inspects repository evidence under explicit correctness/regression review instructions. - Independence boundary: separate child process/model context, role-specific instructions, read-only execution boundary, no nested delegation and no file-editing role. - Who acts on findings: the primary DSCode agent receives the reviewer output in the delegate result and owns subsequent integration/fix/verification decisions.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material outside-and-future intelligence/adaptation loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: project instructions, skills, model/provider configuration, sessions and diagnostics enrich future operation, but no autonomous prospective environmental-modeling and persistent adaptation-selection loop was reconstructed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions; Agent Skills; MCP/hooks; model/provider selection; durable sessions; context compaction; diagnostics. - Closure path: not applicable; no external/future distinction → adaptation-option generation → autonomous selection → persistent capability/strategy change → later operation closure was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: generic ability to edit project instructions/configuration or use skills is not by itself S4. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: downstream workflows can use DSCode to modify durable project configuration; that generic capability is not credited as a closed first-party S4 loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: permission modes, sandbox/network boundaries, project trust, hooks and project instructions constrain operation, but they are configured operating policy rather than identity-level governance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue. - Decision owner: user/operator/project configuration for the relevant constraints. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan/ask/auto/full permissions; OS/Docker sandbox; network gating; project trust; approvals; AGENTS/CLAUDE instructions; hooks. - Closure path: not applicable at S5 level; no identity/policy conflict → legitimate ultimate authority → authoritative decision → returned organizational operation loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: models operate within selected policy; deterministic enforcement does not become S5 by being strong. - Evidence: README.md; packages/core/src/dscode-extension.ts; packages/core/src/access.ts. - Basis: explicit + structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a broader organization can govern DSCode through these controls, but that external parent is not imported without function-specific closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e2aa232f6151393ed27c6eea610c75185389a941/assessments/dscode.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e2aa232f6151393ed27c6eea610c75185389a941\">e2aa232 &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
